WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.780
- Welcome to the Huberman Lab Podcast,

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:04.890
where we discuss science
and science based tools

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:05.913
for everyday life.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:09.990
I'm Andrew Huberman

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:12.690
and I'm a professor of
neurobiology and ophthalmology

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.430
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:17.520
Today we are discussing
focus and concentration

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.700
and tools for improving your
focus and concentration.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.530
This is a topic that
I've covered previously

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:25.290
on the Huberman Lab Podcast,
but in different contexts.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.930
For instance, we had a very
popular episode on ADHD,

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.090
attention deficit hyperactivity disorder,

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:32.100
which of course relates
to the topic of focus

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:33.480
and how to improve focus.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.480
We've also talked a lot about
dopamine motivation and drive.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.580
In fact, that's the title of your previous

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.877
also quite popular
episode of this podcast,

00:00:40.877 --> 00:00:45.300
but never before have we had a
single episode solely devoted

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.910
to the tools to improve
focus and concentration.

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:49.950
The rationale for recording this episode

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.560
is to provide people one
location where they can go

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.480
and quickly access the specific tools

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.460
for increasing focus and concentration

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.040
that are known to be
the most powerful tools

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.750
and the most up to date tools.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.540
In fact, today's episode is
going to include description

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.400
of several peer reviewed studies

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:06.540
and the tools that emerge

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.370
from those scientific
peer reviewed studies

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:09.690
that point to new,

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.030
and fortunately, even briefer protocols

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:13.350
than I've described before.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.140
So what I'm basically
describing here is tools

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.060
that in a very short amount of time

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.660
will allow you to significantly increase

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:22.560
your focus and concentration abilities.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.600
Those tools will include behavioral tools,

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.000
nutrition based tools,
supplement based tools,

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.920
brain machine interface based tools,

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.410
and for those of you that
are working with a physician,

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.120
prescription drug tools.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.160
Today's episode ought to benefit anybody,

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.350
young or old or anything in between

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.990
whether or not you have ADHD or not.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:41.730
Today's episode is going to give you tools

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.560
that you can apply in your daily life,

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.630
most all of them are completely zero cost

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.910
and those tools will allow you

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.650
to tap into the neurochemistry

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.780
and the neurocircuits
within your brain and body

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.420
that peer-reviewed
science has reliably shown

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.040
can significantly improve

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.470
your focus and concentration abilities.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:01:59.400
Just to give you a little teaser

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.080
of the kinds of tools that
I'm going to provide you

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:02.310
on today's episode,

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.110
a previous guest on the
Huberman Lab Podcast

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:05.400
was Dr. Wendy Suzuki.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.670
Dr. Suzuki is a professor of
psychology and neuroscience

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:12.090
at New York University, or NYU
as it's commonly referred to.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.810
She's also the Dean of
Arts and Sciences at NYU.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:18.210
Her laboratory made a
very important discovery,

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.940
which was that a very brief, just 12,

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.520
actually 13 if you really count the intro,

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.010
but 13 minute daily meditation

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.620
performed for a period
of about eight weeks

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:30.030
significantly increased

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.160
people's focus and
concentration abilities.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.410
And the great news is you
didn't need all eight weeks,

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.540
it was just that's how long
that you ran the study.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:37.710
So during today's episode,

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:39.840
I will describe that protocol in detail.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.820
I'll also provide you an even briefer

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:44.430
alternative to that
protocol that you can use,

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:45.810
if for instance, you find yourself

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:47.610
with only three minutes or four minutes

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.770
or five minutes a day to meditate.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.930
The great news is there's
quality peer reviewed science

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:54.150
to support that form of meditation

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:56.820
for improving focus and concentration.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.910
And that falls under the
bin of these zero cost tools

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:02.010
that you can really use to
tap into the neurochemistry

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:03.810
and neurocircuits that really allow you

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:05.490
to take control of your
cognitive abilities

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.500
and improve them over time.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:09.120
I'll also provide you important details

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:10.680
about that protocol and other protocols.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:11.940
For instance,

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.100
contrary to popular belief, it is not,

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:15.960
I repeat it is not a good idea

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:18.360
to do a focused based meditation

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.850
within the four hours before bedtime.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:21.683
Many people,

00:03:21.683 --> 00:03:23.580
including some of the
subjects in that study

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:25.658
performed by the Suzuki lab,

00:03:25.658 --> 00:03:30.030
found that when they did a
focusing meditation protocol,

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.620
even if it was very calming,

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.860
it led to difficulties in
falling and staying asleep.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.600
So that runs counter to
a lot of what we've heard

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.340
about meditation being great for sleep.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.200
It turns out meditation
might be great for sleep,

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.780
it certainly is great for
improving focus capacity,

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:44.670
but I highly recommend that
if you're going to apply

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:46.530
a focus meditation tool

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:48.840
in order to improve your
focus and concentration,

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.610
that you make sure that that's performed

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:53.580
not within four hours prior to bedtime.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:54.870
So that's just a brief example

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:56.820
of the sorts of tools and protocols

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.530
and details about the tools and protocols

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.540
that I'll provide on today's episode.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.760
I should mention that
we have provided links

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:04.020
