that is a visual image that
you can put in your mind,

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:04.440
that will help you incorporate
the tools that I'll provide

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:06.570
and that generally will
help you understand

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:10.470
at a mechanistic level how
focus and concentration work.

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:13.650
So what I want you to imagine is an arrow.

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:15.810
And an arrow of course, has an arrowhead

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:18.180
and it has the shaft of that arrow.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:20.400
And in the context of the neuroscience

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:22.380
of focus and concentration,

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:24.210
the neurochemical system

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:26.760
that really represents
the shaft of that arrow,

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:29.460
right, the straight line is epinephrine,

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:30.750
also called adrenaline.

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:31.583
And today,

00:10:31.583 --> 00:10:34.200
I'll refer to adrenaline and
epinephrine interchangeably.

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:36.480
Turns out that epinephrine/adrenaline

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:39.330
are released within your
brain from a little location,

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:41.280
a little cluster of neurons
called locus coeruleus,

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:43.320
but you do not need to remember that name,

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:45.977
and from your body from
the adrenal glands.

00:10:45.977 --> 00:10:50.610
And the release of epinephrine
from those two locations

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:54.420
overall increases energy,
it increases alertness.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:57.510
It does not alone increase focus, okay?

00:10:57.510 --> 00:11:00.750
So the reason I've assigned epinephrine,

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:02.640
adrenaline as the shaft of the arrow

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:05.580
is that if focus is the arrow,

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:08.640
there is no focus without epinephrine.

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:09.960
So things,

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:12.780
whether or not they're
behavioral or psychological

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:16.800
or supplements or drugs
that increase epinephrine

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:20.220
allow focus to occur, they
are necessary for focus,

00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:22.560
but they are not sufficient for focus,

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:23.640
that is they are required

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:26.220
but they are not enough to create focus.

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:28.680
So we're going to need
epinephrine in the equation.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:33.210
Without epinephrine, there
is no focus or concentration.

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:37.560
Now the arrowhead on this
metaphorical arrowhead

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:39.660
that represents focus and concentration

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:41.250
is going to be represented

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:44.370
or related to the
mechanisms of acetylcholine,

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:46.050
a different neurochemical

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:47.520
that also exists in the brain and body.

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:48.353
In fact, in the body,

00:11:48.353 --> 00:11:49.186
it's responsible

00:11:49.186 --> 00:11:51.570
for the contraction and
movement of your muscles.

00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:53.340
But today, we're talking
about acetylcholine

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:54.330
not in that context,

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:57.060
but rather in the context of
its release within the brain.

00:11:57.060 --> 00:11:58.110
Acetylcholine is released

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:00.060
from a couple of different
locations in the brain

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:03.390
and the best way to think about
it is it's like a spotlight.

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:06.510
It highlights specific
neurons, nerve cells

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:09.000
that should be active, or
more active I should say,

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:10.890
than the other neurons in the environment.

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:15.420
So the reason I've assigned
the arrowhead to acetylcholine

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:17.250
and acetylcholine to the arrowhead

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:20.910
is that if you have an arrow
with a very big arrowhead

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:22.530
that's really broad, really broad,

00:12:22.530 --> 00:12:24.600
imagine a mile wide arrowhead,

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:26.520
that's not very focused
on any one location,

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:29.190
it's not really pointing
to any one location, is it?

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:33.450
But with a narrow, really
tightly focused arrowhead,

00:12:33.450 --> 00:12:35.430
well, that's focused on one location.

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:37.710
So we have alertness, epinephrine,

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:40.590
and then we have the actual direction

00:12:40.590 --> 00:12:43.650
in which our concentration
and focus is placed,

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:44.670
and that's,

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:47.700
at least in this mental model
I'm creating, acetylcholine.

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:51.930
And then in order to have ongoing focus,

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:53.460
we need another neurochemical.

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:56.580
And it turns out that that
third neurochemical is dopamine,

00:12:56.580 --> 00:13:00.000
a molecule often associated
with pleasure and reward,

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:03.210
but it's really the
molecule of motivation.

00:13:03.210 --> 00:13:04.920
So here, I want you to
imagine in your mind,

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:06.570
an arrow with an arrowhead,

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:08.310
think acetylcholine in the arrowhead,

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:10.590
a shaft or a line behind that arrowhead,

00:13:10.590 --> 00:13:13.080
which is epinephrine
also called adrenaline.

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:14.130
And then behind it,

00:13:14.130 --> 00:13:17.220
a sort of an engine that keeps
that focus moving forward,

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:19.680
right because we don't just
want to be focused for a moment,

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:22.440
we want to be able to focus
for 10 minutes or for an hour

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:24.150
or maybe even for two hours.

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:26.130
Turns out there's an
optimal duration to focus,

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:28.020
I'll teach you that in just a little bit,

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:29.940
but these three neurochemicals together,

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:33.420
acetylcholine, epinephrine
and dopamine really allow you

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:37.830
to get focused, to focus very precisely,

00:13:37.830 --> 00:13:40.620
and in fact, increasingly
precisely over time

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:42.510
to really narrow and narrow
and narrow your focus

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:45.390
progressively within a
single about of focus

00:13:45.390 --> 00:13:46.737
and to continue to do that

00:13:46.737 --> 00:13:49.560
and to be able to do that
repeatedly whenever you want.

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:51.780
So here, I'm purely talking in metaphor

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:53.940
and in models and mental models of arrows,

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:54.810
but in a moment or two,

00:13:54.810 --> 00:13:57.150
I'll start transitioning
to discussing tools

00:13:57.150 --> 00:13:58.170
in which I'll talk about

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:00.150
increasing dopamine and acetylcholine

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:02.520
or increasing epinephrine and dopamine

00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:05.220
in various combinations
with various approaches.

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:07.320
And what I'd like you to conceptualize

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:09.090
is how those are contributing

00:14:09.090 --> 00:14:11.730
to creating a very narrowly pointed arrow

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:12.780
that has the capacity

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:14.730
to continue moving forward over and over

00:14:14.730 --> 00:14:18.570
so that you can focus as
sharply and as long as you like.

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:19.590
And of course, for those of you

00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:21.600
that want to get really down in the weeds

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:22.770
of how dopamine works,

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:24.030
we have an entire episode

00:14:24.030 --> 00:14:25.620
about dopamine motivation and drive

00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:27.960
that really gets into
neurotransmitter release

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:30.030
and dopamine baselines and thresholds

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:31.620
and all of that sort of thing.

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:34.500
We also have episodes on focus,

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:36.600
much longer episodes,
I should say on focus

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:39.150
that incorporate a lot of
the biology of acetylcholine.

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:40.620
It turns out acetylcholine
is also involved

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:42.150
in neuroplasticity, et cetera,

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:44.607
and epinephrine of
course, relates to stress

00:14:44.607 --> 00:14:46.890
and our capacity to deal
with and buffer stress

00:14:46.890 --> 00:14:48.390
and on and on.

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:50.220
Those episodes are all available to you

00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:52.950
in their long form at hubermanlab.com,

00:14:52.950 --> 00:14:54.030
you can find them very easily.

00:14:54.030 --> 00:14:54.920
They are all timestamped

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:56.880
so you can navigate to
the particular topics

00:14:56.880 --> 00:14:57.960
most of interest to you.

00:14:57.960 --> 00:15:00.000
I mention this all not as a diversion

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.410
from what we want to cover today,

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:02.730
but I know that some of you are hungry

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:04.170
for a lot more mechanism,

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:06.870
but today's episode is really
mainly focused on the tools,

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:09.210
I will of course touch on mechanism,

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:11.430
but if you really want to do
the deep dive on mechanism,

00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:13.020
go to hubermanlab.com

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:15.060
and you'll have more
than you ever could want

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:16.800
about those mechanisms.

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:20.370
Let's jump into the tools
for concentration and focus.

00:15:20.370 --> 00:15:23.700
If you want to think
about tools of any kind

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:26.910
to modify your biology
or physiology in any way,

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:28.200
whether or not it's for cognitive function

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:29.700
or you want to get better at exercising,

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:30.570
or you want to build muscle,

00:15:30.570 --> 00:15:32.160
or you want to improve your hormones,

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:35.550
you need to think and understand tools

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:38.940
in the context of
modulation and mediation.

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:39.840
What do I mean by that?

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:42.480
Well, it's quite simple really.

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:46.560
Mediation is how specific
types of chemicals and cells

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:49.860
and circuits and organs
control very specific things

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:51.090
in your brain and body

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:52.770
whereas modulation

00:15:52.770 --> 00:15:55.920
is the ability of chemicals
and cells and circuits

00:15:55.920 --> 00:15:58.950
to adjust how different things change,

00:15:58.950 --> 00:16:01.020
how different things work
in your brain and body,

00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:02.670
but to do it more broadly.

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:03.503
What do I mean by this?

00:16:03.503 --> 00:16:04.800
Let me give an example.

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:07.560
For instance, I'm going to tell you now

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:10.020
that one of the most important things

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:14.040
to build and maintain your
focus and concentration

00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:17.340
is to optimize your sleeping behavior,

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:19.709
that is to get enough quality sleep

00:16:19.709 --> 00:16:22.080
I would say 80% of the
nights of your life.

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:23.850
Not everyone can get optimal sleep

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:25.230
100% of the nights of their life,

00:16:25.230 --> 00:16:28.290
nobody, truly nobody achieves that.

00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:30.450
However, sleep has been shown

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:33.750
to relate to cognitive
performance, physical performance,

00:16:33.750 --> 00:16:36.330
hormone output, and so many other things,

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:38.400
including immune system function.

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:42.510
What we can reliably say
is that sleep modulates

00:16:42.510 --> 00:16:45.900
just about every process
in your brain and body.

00:16:45.900 --> 00:16:47.130
So you have to get great sleep.

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:50.250
There is simply no tool
that's going to allow you

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:53.040
to overcome chronic sleep deprivation

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:54.570
and allow you to remain focused,

00:16:54.570 --> 00:16:58.830
no pill, no device, no supplement,
no protocol whatsoever.

00:16:58.830 --> 00:17:00.990
There are tools to overcome one night

00:17:00.990 --> 00:17:02.580
or maybe two nights of sleep deprivation

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:04.050
and we'll talk about those,

00:17:04.050 --> 00:17:06.240
but at a fundamental level,

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:07.590
we need to do the things

00:17:07.590 --> 00:17:11.220
that modulate our focus and
attention in powerful ways

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:13.200
and sleep really is that thing.

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:16.170
So we've done two episodes,
one called "Master Your Sleep"

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:18.930
and the other episode
is "Perfect Your Sleep."

00:17:18.930 --> 00:17:19.763
The "Perfect Your Sleep"

00:17:19.763 --> 00:17:20.970
is a little bit more like this episode,

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:22.027
more focused on protocols.

00:17:22.027 --> 00:17:24.450
"Master Your Sleep" includes
protocols and mechanism.

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:26.220
Again, you can find
those at hubermanlab.com.

00:17:26.220 --> 00:17:28.020
We also have a sleep toolkit,

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:29.430
a distilled list of things to do

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:30.613
in order to optimize your sleep.

00:17:30.613 --> 00:17:32.820
I highly recommend that you download that.

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:34.050
You can go to hubermanlab.com,

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:36.180
go to the Neural Network
Newsletter, it is listed there.

00:17:36.180 --> 00:17:37.680
If you want you can sign
up for the newsletter,

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:38.580
but you don't have to,

00:17:38.580 --> 00:17:41.100
you can simply download
the PDF of that toolkit

00:17:41.100 --> 00:17:42.900
for zero cost.

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:45.960
Why do I say sleep modulates
focus and attention?

00:17:45.960 --> 00:17:47.910
Well, I'll give an analogy.

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:50.820
If right now someone pulled
a fire alarm in this building

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:52.140
or if we had a fire in this building,

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:54.120
my attention would drift,

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:56.030
it would not be on recording this podcast,

00:17:56.030 --> 00:17:57.810
it would be on something else.

00:17:57.810 --> 00:18:01.110
But would I say that the fire
alarm mediates attention?

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:03.510
I mean fire alarms are not
really involved in attention.

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:06.660
No, rather they modulate my attention.

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:08.910
The noise in the room
modulates my attention.

00:18:08.910 --> 00:18:10.320
That's quite a bit different

00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:11.640
than a tool that I'll provide later

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:13.230
and I'll just give you
a little hint of now,

00:18:13.230 --> 00:18:14.580
in fact, I'll give it to you now,

00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:17.700
which is that 40 HZ binaural beats

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:20.040
have been shown in a number
of peer reviewed studies

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:22.410
to increase focus and concentration.

00:18:22.410 --> 00:18:25.350
And if you'd like to
access 40 HZ binaural beats

00:18:25.350 --> 00:18:27.750
in order to improve your
focus and concentration,

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:30.180
you can do that, you can
actually get it at zero cost.

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:32.220
You can go into the app store,

00:18:32.220 --> 00:18:34.020
for instance, the Apple App Store,

00:18:34.020 --> 00:18:35.670
this is also available for Android phone,

00:18:35.670 --> 00:18:37.200
there's an app called BrainWave,

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:39.570
and you can go there,
you can dial in 40 HZ

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:41.490
and it'll play these binaural beats.

00:18:41.490 --> 00:18:43.920
It's been shown in multiple
quality peer reviewed studies

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:46.260
that playing a pattern of
sound waves to one ear,

00:18:46.260 --> 00:18:47.310
do, do, do, do, do,

00:18:47.310 --> 00:18:49.650
and the other ear, which is
slightly offset in frequency,

00:18:49.650 --> 00:18:51.030
meaning not quite the same frequencies,

00:18:51.030 --> 00:18:52.590
so more like do, do, do, do,

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:54.240
that that combination of frequencies

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:56.340
played to the different ears,

00:18:56.340 --> 00:18:59.310
actually get integrated
within deep brain centers

00:18:59.310 --> 00:19:01.440
and can increase focus and concentration

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:03.990
in part by increasing levels

00:19:03.990 --> 00:19:06.360
of the neurochemical
dopamine and acetylcholine,

00:19:06.360 --> 00:19:07.740
which we talked about a little bit earlier

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:09.900
in this arrow model of focus.

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:12.870
So we'll provide a link to that app,

00:19:12.870 --> 00:19:15.900
I don't have any relationship
to that app I should mention,

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:16.770
but it's an excellent one,

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:18.150
it's one that I've used for many years.

00:19:18.150 --> 00:19:20.580
There are also additional
functions within the app,

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:22.680
such as for sleep and for other things,

00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:27.420
but the 40 HZ, 4-0-h-z
is the way it reads out,

00:19:27.420 --> 00:19:28.830
40 HZ stimulation

00:19:28.830 --> 00:19:31.470
has been shown to improve
focus and concentration.

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:33.600
Here is my recommendation
and the way that I use it.

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:36.330
I would not use 40 HZ binaural beats

00:19:36.330 --> 00:19:38.640
every time I'm doing a about of work.

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:39.473
What I tend to do

00:19:39.473 --> 00:19:43.050
is use it for about five
minutes prior to that work

00:19:43.050 --> 00:19:45.150
and then turn it off and then do the work

00:19:45.150 --> 00:19:47.790
and I'll talk about other
tools to use during that work,

00:19:47.790 --> 00:19:48.990
whether or not it's reading or math

00:19:48.990 --> 00:19:51.330
or even just emailing or
something where I require

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:53.059
a bunch of focus for a while.

00:19:53.059 --> 00:19:56.340
However, there are times
in which I'm in an area

00:19:56.340 --> 00:19:57.390
or I'm in a state of mind

00:19:57.390 --> 00:19:58.620
where I'm feeling very distractable

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:00.660
and then I'll keep the
40 HZ binaural beats on

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:01.740
the entire time

00:20:01.740 --> 00:20:04.260
I'm doing that about of cognitive work.

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:06.360
I'll also sometimes use
the 40 HZ binaural beats

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:07.410
prior to a workout,

00:20:07.410 --> 00:20:08.400
in particular weight workouts,

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:09.870
where I really want to be able to focus on

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:11.490
and contract specific muscles.

00:20:11.490 --> 00:20:12.990
So it's a very useful tool,

00:20:12.990 --> 00:20:15.630
again, supported by quality
peer reviewed science,

00:20:15.630 --> 00:20:16.530
zero cost,

00:20:16.530 --> 00:20:18.750
available out there not
just in the BrainWave app

00:20:18.750 --> 00:20:20.520
but in multiple apps.

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:22.628
I think many of you will benefit from it.

00:20:22.628 --> 00:20:25.320
Some of you might not
experience it immediately

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:28.410
as a total dropping into a tunnel of focus

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:29.670
in the same way that you might

00:20:29.670 --> 00:20:32.310
with say the sorts of neurochemicals

00:20:32.310 --> 00:20:33.180
that we'll talk about later,

00:20:33.180 --> 00:20:35.310
like Alpha-GPC and some
of these other things

00:20:35.310 --> 00:20:37.590
that change neurochemicals directly,

00:20:37.590 --> 00:20:39.990
but nonetheless 40 HZ binaural beats

00:20:39.990 --> 00:20:41.340
are a very powerful tool,

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:43.590
again, zero cost non-pharmacologic tool

00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:45.450
that tap into your own endogenous,

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:49.050
meaning within you or
exists within you physiology

00:20:49.050 --> 00:20:50.640
in order to increase acetylcholine

00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:51.960
and some other neurochemicals

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:54.600
and they have been shown
to work quite well.

00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:56.670
Okay so assuming that
you are sleeping well

00:20:56.670 --> 00:20:58.020
80% of the nights of your life

00:20:58.020 --> 00:21:01.740
or at least working on the
various protocols and tools

00:21:01.740 --> 00:21:04.530
to sleep well and sufficiently long

00:21:04.530 --> 00:21:06.480
80% of the nights of your life,

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:09.420
and you are interested in additional tools

00:21:09.420 --> 00:21:11.040
that are sound based

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:14.460
in order to improve your ability
to concentrate and focus,

00:21:14.460 --> 00:21:17.730
there are quality peer reviewed
studies supporting the idea

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:20.310
that white noise or pink noise,

00:21:20.310 --> 00:21:21.143
and believe it or not,

00:21:21.143 --> 00:21:22.110
there is something called pink noise,

00:21:22.110 --> 00:21:25.200
it has to do with the
specific frequencies of sound

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:27.236
that are in the noise,

00:21:27.236 --> 00:21:29.340
well white noise and pink noise

00:21:29.340 --> 00:21:33.360
have been shown to not
improve concentration per se,

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:34.980
but to improve people's ability

00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:37.890
to transition into concentrated states.

00:21:37.890 --> 00:21:40.050
So I don't tend to use
white noise and pink noise

00:21:40.050 --> 00:21:42.390
while I work, but I know a
number of people that do.

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:44.737
I know people that also use
what's called brown noise.

00:21:44.737 --> 00:21:46.020
The folks I know

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:48.510
from the engineering and
computer science side

00:21:48.510 --> 00:21:49.830
get really into these details

00:21:49.830 --> 00:21:51.660
of white noise, pink noise, brown noise.

00:21:51.660 --> 00:21:54.120
You can find white noise,
pink noise or brown noise

00:21:54.120 --> 00:21:57.270
and listen to it through
headphones or in the room.

00:21:57.270 --> 00:21:59.610
There is indeed some data

00:21:59.610 --> 00:22:01.500
to support the fact that white noise,

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:03.570
and to some extent, pink
noise and brown noise

00:22:03.570 --> 00:22:05.640
can support the release of
particular neurochemicals,

00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:09.270
but more data showing that
they can amplify the activity

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:11.910
of neurons in the so-called
prefrontal cortex,

00:22:11.910 --> 00:22:14.880
this front area, sort of the
bumper behind your forehead,

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:18.030
that is directly related to your ability

00:22:18.030 --> 00:22:19.680
to direct your own focus

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:21.960
and remain focused on certain things.

00:22:21.960 --> 00:22:23.970
So you have the option

00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:26.550
of either using binaural beats before,

00:22:26.550 --> 00:22:29.340
but not during your work,
that is 40 HZ binaural beats,

00:22:29.340 --> 00:22:33.990
or 40 HZ binaural beats
throughout your attempt to focus.

00:22:33.990 --> 00:22:36.870
You also have the option of
not using binaural beats,

00:22:36.870 --> 00:22:39.420
but using white noise,
pink noise or brown noise.

00:22:39.420 --> 00:22:41.490
Again, there are a lot of zero cost apps,

00:22:41.490 --> 00:22:44.190
you can find also white noise,
pink noise and brown noise

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:45.330
on YouTube.

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:46.560
Again, these are tools

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:49.680
that really have been shown
over and over in humans

00:22:49.680 --> 00:22:52.200
to allow people to focus with more depth

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:55.530
and to decrease the
transition time into focus.

00:22:55.530 --> 00:22:56.610
This is a really key point.

00:22:56.610 --> 00:22:58.050
A lot of people are challenged

00:22:58.050 --> 00:23:00.296
with getting into a mode of focus.

00:23:00.296 --> 00:23:02.910
None of us, however, should be expected

00:23:02.910 --> 00:23:05.760
to just sit down and drop
directly into a state of focus.

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:09.600
I think that's completely an
unfair request of ourselves.

00:23:09.600 --> 00:23:10.433
I mean, for instance,

00:23:10.433 --> 00:23:12.480
you wouldn't expect yourself
to go out on the track

00:23:12.480 --> 00:23:14.250
or go out for a run and not warm up.

00:23:14.250 --> 00:23:15.360
You might jog for a few minutes

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:16.860
or even walk before you would jog

00:23:16.860 --> 00:23:18.390
and then jog before you would run,

00:23:18.390 --> 00:23:20.010
right, I would hope you would do that.

00:23:20.010 --> 00:23:21.750
And if you're doing resistance training,

00:23:21.750 --> 00:23:23.790
I doubt that you go over and
load up the bar or the machine

00:23:23.790 --> 00:23:25.380
with the maximum amount of
weight that you can move

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:26.280
and then just drop right into that,

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:27.150
you always do a warmup.

00:23:27.150 --> 00:23:29.100
And I think it's very
important to understand

00:23:29.100 --> 00:23:30.780
mental work, focus and concentration

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:32.640
as requiring that warmup.

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:34.140
What is that warmup?

00:23:34.140 --> 00:23:36.360
Well, you know what that warmup is.

00:23:36.360 --> 00:23:39.150
That warmup is the ramping up

00:23:39.150 --> 00:23:43.500
or the increase of
epinephrine, adrenaline,

00:23:43.500 --> 00:23:45.930
acetylcholine, and dopamine.

00:23:45.930 --> 00:23:47.610
Right, the way that neurochemicals work

00:23:47.610 --> 00:23:49.500
is we don't just get to
flip switches in our brain

00:23:49.500 --> 00:23:51.150
because we decide to, that's a fantasy.

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:53.160
That's sort of the "Limitless" movie

00:23:53.160 --> 00:23:55.320
or movies and ideas

00:23:55.320 --> 00:23:57.000
that suddenly you're going
to flip a switch on your arm

00:23:57.000 --> 00:23:58.830
and all of a sudden you're
going to be in a laser focus,

00:23:58.830 --> 00:24:01.410
that is just not the way that
your nervous system works.

00:24:01.410 --> 00:24:03.810
There's a gradual dropping into any state,

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:05.070
whether or not that state is sleep,

00:24:05.070 --> 00:24:06.750
right, you go from shallow
sleep to deep sleep

00:24:06.750 --> 00:24:08.052
and then out eventually.

00:24:08.052 --> 00:24:11.850
Focus too, you go from shallow focus

00:24:11.850 --> 00:24:13.740
to increasingly deep focus.

00:24:13.740 --> 00:24:18.690
That is, in our metaphor of
the arrow, it's very broad,

00:24:18.690 --> 00:24:19.800
it's pointed at a lot of things.

00:24:19.800 --> 00:24:21.510
And over time as we drop into focus,

00:24:21.510 --> 00:24:24.210
that arrow is narrowing and
narrowing and narrowing.

00:24:24.210 --> 00:24:26.820
In fact, probably better
to think about it narrowing

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:29.909
and then sometimes oscillating
and getting wider again.

00:24:29.909 --> 00:24:31.740
We might hear something down the hallway

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:34.230
or more typically our phone
will buzz or we'll think,

00:24:34.230 --> 00:24:35.520
oh, I wonder what so and so is doing

00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:37.620
or I had to contact them about something.

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:39.900
Your focus is dynamic,

00:24:39.900 --> 00:24:41.700
it is not what we call a step function.

00:24:41.700 --> 00:24:43.590
It's not like you go
from unfocused to focused

00:24:43.590 --> 00:24:45.900
and then you drop into your maximal focus.

00:24:45.900 --> 00:24:48.060
By understanding that it's dynamic,

00:24:48.060 --> 00:24:50.310
by understanding that you
are going to be continually

00:24:50.310 --> 00:24:51.360
going in and out

00:24:51.360 --> 00:24:55.526
of progressively but
varying levels of focus,

00:24:55.526 --> 00:24:58.890
you will greatly release
the pressure on yourself

00:24:58.890 --> 00:25:02.160
to feel focused all the
time when you want to be.

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:02.993
This is very key.

00:25:02.993 --> 00:25:05.520
People who are very good
at focusing understand this

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:09.270
and understand that they
can't expect themselves

00:25:09.270 --> 00:25:10.560
to just immediately focus

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:13.050
and then snap into or out of focus.

00:25:13.050 --> 00:25:14.640
Okay so be patient with yourself

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:17.820
and also understand that
focus is an ability,

00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:19.920
that you can improve your ability to focus

00:25:19.920 --> 00:25:22.710
by engaging the neural
circuits responsible for focus

00:25:22.710 --> 00:25:25.620
repeatedly over time through
so-called neuroplasticity,

00:25:25.620 --> 00:25:27.210
the ability of your nervous system

00:25:27.210 --> 00:25:28.860
to change in response to experience.

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:30.660
And that has a couple
of different components,

00:25:30.660 --> 00:25:31.980
but put very simply,

00:25:31.980 --> 00:25:34.680
what we repeat gets etched
into our nervous system

00:25:34.680 --> 00:25:36.510
and becomes easier over time.

00:25:36.510 --> 00:25:39.990
And the more emotionally important

00:25:39.990 --> 00:25:41.670
or vital something feels to us,

00:25:41.670 --> 00:25:43.560
the more likely it is to
trigger neuroplasticity.

00:25:43.560 --> 00:25:44.520
We're going to talk a little bit more

00:25:44.520 --> 00:25:46.620
about how to increase neural
circuits for focus later,

00:25:46.620 --> 00:25:48.330
but right now, what you have in hand

00:25:48.330 --> 00:25:49.920
is the key importance of sleep

00:25:49.920 --> 00:25:52.680
and I again will direct
you to hubermanlab.com

00:25:52.680 --> 00:25:53.820
and the Neural Network Newsletter

00:25:53.820 --> 00:25:57.420
to really work on optimizing your sleep.

00:25:57.420 --> 00:26:01.080
We've also got two
auditory sound based tools

00:26:01.080 --> 00:26:01.920
for improving focus,

00:26:01.920 --> 00:26:05.010
there's 40 HZ binaural
beats used before or during

00:26:05.010 --> 00:26:06.600
bouts of focus and concentration.

00:26:06.600 --> 00:26:09.300
And if you don't like
those, or even if you do,

00:26:09.300 --> 00:26:10.410
you might alternate them with

00:26:10.410 --> 00:26:13.050
or occasionally use white noise,
pink noise or brown noise,

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:15.240
also readily available at zero cost.

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:18.300
A question I often get is how
long should I try to focus?

00:26:18.300 --> 00:26:20.820
Well, the research literature
point to the key importance

00:26:20.820 --> 00:26:22.530
of so-called ultradian cycles.

00:26:22.530 --> 00:26:25.290
You've all probably
heard of circadian cycles

00:26:25.290 --> 00:26:29.160
or circadian biology,
circa, the day, circadian,

00:26:29.160 --> 00:26:30.720
is about 24 hour cycle.

00:26:30.720 --> 00:26:33.810
Well, our brain and body
operate within that day

00:26:33.810 --> 00:26:36.160
or within each and every day I should say

00:26:36.160 --> 00:26:39.360
with 90 minute ultradian cycles.

00:26:39.360 --> 00:26:40.650
So my suggestion would be

00:26:40.650 --> 00:26:42.440
anytime you're going to
sit down and try and focus

00:26:42.440 --> 00:26:44.520
or you're going to try
and do a focused about

00:26:44.520 --> 00:26:47.670
of physical exercise or skill
learning or musical learning,

00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:49.320
or maybe you're even just
having a conversation,

00:26:49.320 --> 00:26:50.280
maybe you're a therapist

00:26:50.280 --> 00:26:52.020
or you're attending therapy or a class,

00:26:52.020 --> 00:26:53.130
how long should it be?

00:26:53.130 --> 00:26:56.010
And the ideal duration
is about 90 minutes,

00:26:56.010 --> 00:26:57.570
not exactly 90 minutes,

00:26:57.570 --> 00:27:00.750
but we can reliably
say 90 minutes or less,

00:27:00.750 --> 00:27:02.850
okay, it doesn't have to
be the full 90 minutes.

00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:05.460
But trying to push yourself
to be able to drop into

00:27:05.460 --> 00:27:07.560
two hours of focus or
three hours of focus,

00:27:07.560 --> 00:27:11.190
while possible is not really in line

00:27:11.190 --> 00:27:13.230
with what we know about
the underlying biology.

00:27:13.230 --> 00:27:15.090
Everything from our sleep states

00:27:15.090 --> 00:27:17.820
or the different stages of
sleep and our waking states

00:27:17.820 --> 00:27:20.070
is divided into these 90 minute cycles

00:27:20.070 --> 00:27:21.510
or so-called ultradian cycles.

00:27:21.510 --> 00:27:24.210
So what I like to do is
set a timer for 90 minutes.

00:27:24.210 --> 00:27:26.190
I acknowledge and accept the fact

00:27:26.190 --> 00:27:27.900
that under most conditions

00:27:27.900 --> 00:27:29.970
unless I'm really pressed for a deadline

00:27:29.970 --> 00:27:32.130
and I'm optimally caffeinated, et cetera,

00:27:32.130 --> 00:27:34.620
the first five to 10
minutes of that 90 minutes

00:27:34.620 --> 00:27:37.110
are a transition time, it's
like the warm up for focus,

00:27:37.110 --> 00:27:39.090
but I do include it in that 90 minutes.

00:27:39.090 --> 00:27:41.054
And then I really try and drop into

00:27:41.054 --> 00:27:44.490
doing focused mental work
or learning of some sort,

00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:47.130
again, this could be physical
as well, motor skill learning

00:27:47.130 --> 00:27:49.680
or anything, we're running or
lifting weights, et cetera,

00:27:49.680 --> 00:27:51.270
and really try and drop into that

00:27:51.270 --> 00:27:52.530
across the full 90 minutes,

00:27:52.530 --> 00:27:54.630
again accepting the fact,

00:27:54.630 --> 00:27:55.770
okay it's not just an idea,

00:27:55.770 --> 00:27:58.800
the fact that occasionally
our focus will flicker,

00:27:58.800 --> 00:28:00.120
it will jump out of focus,

00:28:00.120 --> 00:28:01.670
and then a big part of being able to focus

00:28:01.670 --> 00:28:03.090
is to go back to focusing.

00:28:03.090 --> 00:28:05.400
The way I'd like you to
conceptualize this perhaps

00:28:05.400 --> 00:28:07.830
is that arrowhead suddenly
getting very, very broad,

00:28:07.830 --> 00:28:09.090
that you're focusing on many things

00:28:09.090 --> 00:28:11.913
or that arrow shifts to a
different location in the room.

00:28:11.913 --> 00:28:14.220
The key is to be able to shift it back

00:28:14.220 --> 00:28:16.927
and to narrow it once again
and that's an active process,

00:28:16.927 --> 00:28:20.340
so much so that it requires
a lot of metabolic energy,

00:28:20.340 --> 00:28:25.113
your brain is the chief
consumer of metabolic energy.

00:28:25.113 --> 00:28:26.607
The calories that you consume,

00:28:26.607 --> 00:28:28.170
the so-called basal metabolic rate,

00:28:28.170 --> 00:28:29.250
most of that isn't related

00:28:29.250 --> 00:28:30.690
to movement or heartbeat or breathing,

00:28:30.690 --> 00:28:32.520
it's related to brain function.

00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:35.880
Your brain is a glutton with
respect to caloric need.

00:28:35.880 --> 00:28:37.800
So understand that at
the end of 90 minutes

00:28:37.800 --> 00:28:39.390
or maybe even after 45 minutes,

00:28:39.390 --> 00:28:42.090
you might feel rather
tired or even exhausted.

00:28:42.090 --> 00:28:45.150
And it's very important
that after a about of focus

00:28:45.150 --> 00:28:46.890
that you take at least 10 minutes

00:28:46.890 --> 00:28:48.960
and ideally as long as 30 minutes

00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:51.600
and go through what I
call deliberate defocus.

00:28:51.600 --> 00:28:54.210
You really want to focus
on somewhat menial tasks

00:28:54.210 --> 00:28:55.800
or things that really don't require

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:57.489
a ton of your concentration.

00:28:57.489 --> 00:28:58.920
This is starting to become

00:28:58.920 --> 00:29:00.540
a little bit of a movement out there

00:29:00.540 --> 00:29:03.990
in the kind of pop psychology
and optimization world,

00:29:03.990 --> 00:29:06.240
this idea of not looking at your phone

00:29:06.240 --> 00:29:07.590
as you walk down the hall to the bathroom,

00:29:07.590 --> 00:29:09.120
certainly not looking at
your phone in the bathroom.

00:29:09.120 --> 00:29:10.500
And I should mention, by the way,

00:29:10.500 --> 00:29:12.690
this is a particular annoyance of mine,

00:29:12.690 --> 00:29:14.880
have you noticed that wait times

00:29:14.880 --> 00:29:16.320
for restrooms in public places

00:29:16.320 --> 00:29:18.810
has increased substantially
in the last 10 years?

00:29:18.810 --> 00:29:21.630
The reason for that is
not digestive, okay,

00:29:21.630 --> 00:29:23.160
it's not the gut microbiome,

00:29:23.160 --> 00:29:24.660
I mean it might be the gut microbiome,

00:29:24.660 --> 00:29:25.493
but chances are

00:29:25.493 --> 00:29:27.030
it's because people are on
their phones in the bathroom.

00:29:27.030 --> 00:29:30.030
So you're doing yourself
and everybody else a favor

00:29:30.030 --> 00:29:32.460
by staying off your phone in the restroom,

00:29:32.460 --> 00:29:35.340
staying off your phone
while walking down the hall.

00:29:35.340 --> 00:29:38.430
Try and give yourself some time
to deliberately decompress,

00:29:38.430 --> 00:29:41.190
to let your mental states idle,

00:29:41.190 --> 00:29:43.050
to not be focused on any one thing.

00:29:43.050 --> 00:29:45.150
That period of idling

00:29:45.150 --> 00:29:47.400
is essential for your ability to focus,

00:29:47.400 --> 00:29:48.233
much in the same way

00:29:48.233 --> 00:29:50.760
that rest between sets
of resistance training

00:29:50.760 --> 00:29:52.560
or rest between exercise

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:55.230
is vital to being able
to focus and perform

00:29:55.230 --> 00:29:57.780
during the actual sets or
during the actual bouts

00:29:57.780 --> 00:29:59.190
of running or cycling

00:29:59.190 --> 00:30:02.220
or whatever your particular
form of exercise might be.

00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:04.350
So deliberate decompression is key.

00:30:04.350 --> 00:30:06.780
And I know this is hard because
we're all being drawn in

00:30:06.780 --> 00:30:10.170
by the incredible rich array

00:30:10.170 --> 00:30:11.640
of sensory information available

00:30:11.640 --> 00:30:13.500
on our phones and other devices,

00:30:13.500 --> 00:30:15.390
but I can't emphasize this enough,

00:30:15.390 --> 00:30:17.940
our ability to focus is not just related

00:30:17.940 --> 00:30:20.820
to what happens during
the entry and movement

00:30:20.820 --> 00:30:23.760
through those focused bouts
but after those focused bouts.

00:30:23.760 --> 00:30:25.530
We really need to deliberately decompress.

00:30:25.530 --> 00:30:27.300
And of course, the ultimate decompress,

00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:28.830
the time in which we are not directing

00:30:28.830 --> 00:30:30.840
our thinking interaction is during sleep.

00:30:30.840 --> 00:30:32.250
And so it's no wonder,

00:30:32.250 --> 00:30:34.230
or I should say it holds
together logically,

00:30:34.230 --> 00:30:37.440
that that deep, long lasting duration

00:30:37.440 --> 00:30:39.960
of not controlling where our mind is at

00:30:39.960 --> 00:30:42.480
is in fact the ultimate
form of restoration,

00:30:42.480 --> 00:30:44.310
even if we have very intense dreams.

00:30:44.310 --> 00:30:47.730
So take that period after each
90 minute or less focus about,

00:30:47.730 --> 00:30:48.840
right, remember those focus bouts

00:30:48.840 --> 00:30:50.160
don't have to be full 90 minutes,

00:30:50.160 --> 00:30:51.870
let's say you do 45 minutes of work,

00:30:51.870 --> 00:30:52.703
you're just done with it,

00:30:52.703 --> 00:30:55.320
set it down and go do
something for maybe five, 10,

00:30:55.320 --> 00:30:58.907
maybe even 30 minutes that
is functional for your day,

00:30:58.907 --> 00:31:00.960
right, not just walking around in circles

00:31:00.960 --> 00:31:01.860
or staring up at the sky

00:31:01.860 --> 00:31:03.690
although if you can do that, do that,

00:31:03.690 --> 00:31:05.250
but most of us have other things to do,

00:31:05.250 --> 00:31:06.900
but do things that are rather automatic

00:31:06.900 --> 00:31:08.040
or reflexive for you,

00:31:08.040 --> 00:31:10.440
and try not to do any focused reading,

00:31:10.440 --> 00:31:13.260
try not to bring your vision
into a tight location,

00:31:13.260 --> 00:31:16.410
such as your phone and try
and deliberately decompress,

00:31:16.410 --> 00:31:17.700
because that will allow you

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:20.190
to drop into intense spouts of focus

00:31:20.190 --> 00:31:22.620
again repeatedly and
repeatedly throughout the day.

00:31:22.620 --> 00:31:25.140
I'm often asked how many ultradian cycles

00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:26.760
one can perform throughout the day?

00:31:26.760 --> 00:31:28.740
That depends on how well you've slept,

00:31:28.740 --> 00:31:29.880
how well you are nourished,

00:31:29.880 --> 00:31:31.350
which we'll talk about in a moment,

00:31:31.350 --> 00:31:34.530
and how well trained up
your focus capacity is.

00:31:34.530 --> 00:31:36.714
And here's the paradox,

00:31:36.714 --> 00:31:39.540
if you are very trained at focusing,

00:31:39.540 --> 00:31:41.610
if you're very good at
dropping into focus,

00:31:41.610 --> 00:31:42.780
you're actually going to need

00:31:42.780 --> 00:31:44.967
more deliberate decompression and defocus

00:31:44.967 --> 00:31:47.400
and I recommend only doing about two,

00:31:47.400 --> 00:31:51.090
maybe three deep work sessions per day.

00:31:51.090 --> 00:31:54.120
So not one 90 minute session
than expecting yourself

00:31:54.120 --> 00:31:55.920
to do to another one and
another one, another one,

00:31:55.920 --> 00:31:58.350
but rather one deep
work 90 minute session,

00:31:58.350 --> 00:32:00.090
and maybe another in the afternoon.

00:32:00.090 --> 00:32:01.837
A lot of people get
surprised by this and say,

00:32:01.837 --> 00:32:03.360
"Wait, how many people can afford

00:32:03.360 --> 00:32:04.650
to just work three hours a day?"

00:32:04.650 --> 00:32:06.090
I'm not saying just
work three hours a day.

00:32:06.090 --> 00:32:08.340
I'm really talking about
the hard mental work.

00:32:08.340 --> 00:32:10.764
And again, somewhat paradoxically,

00:32:10.764 --> 00:32:12.600
the more you can concentrate,

00:32:12.600 --> 00:32:14.400
the more deeply you can concentrate,

00:32:14.400 --> 00:32:16.860
the fewer deep work concentration bouts

00:32:16.860 --> 00:32:18.330
you can actually perform each day.

00:32:18.330 --> 00:32:20.460
This makes sense, however,

00:32:20.460 --> 00:32:21.570
if you think about it in the context

00:32:21.570 --> 00:32:22.620
of say resistance training.

00:32:22.620 --> 00:32:25.380
If you are stronger and
stronger in the gym,

00:32:25.380 --> 00:32:26.730
or you're an endurance athlete

00:32:26.730 --> 00:32:30.540
and you can run ultra-marathons,
100 miles or so,

00:32:30.540 --> 00:32:33.750
you are essentially cutting a deeper cut

00:32:33.750 --> 00:32:36.000
into your recovery capacity

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:38.250
than somebody who's not
very skilled at those things

00:32:38.250 --> 00:32:40.710
or can't perform as much intense work.

00:32:40.710 --> 00:32:43.560
So the intensity of the
work scales directly

00:32:43.560 --> 00:32:45.744
with how long you need
to rest after that work.

00:32:45.744 --> 00:32:48.240
I at this stage of my life am pretty good

00:32:48.240 --> 00:32:49.530
at dropping into

00:32:49.530 --> 00:32:51.804
and maintaining focused
bouts of concentration

00:32:51.804 --> 00:32:54.340
provided the landscape
of my life is right,

00:32:54.340 --> 00:32:55.590
you know, I don't have some
burning stressful thing

00:32:55.590 --> 00:32:58.020
that's essential or an
emergency that I'm tending to

00:32:58.020 --> 00:33:00.330
and that I put my phone
away or turn it off,

00:33:00.330 --> 00:33:03.900
I can do three 90 minute
focus bouts per day,

00:33:03.900 --> 00:33:04.920
but that's about it.

00:33:04.920 --> 00:33:06.900
And then in between those focus bouts,

00:33:06.900 --> 00:33:09.270
I'm doing other things
that require less focus.

00:33:09.270 --> 00:33:11.160
Some of you may be able to perform four.

00:33:11.160 --> 00:33:12.180
What I highly recommend

00:33:12.180 --> 00:33:14.675
is that you try doing at least one,

00:33:14.675 --> 00:33:17.712
that is one 90 minute or less

00:33:17.712 --> 00:33:21.480
about of focused
concentrated work per day,

00:33:21.480 --> 00:33:23.070
and yes, that means the weekends too.

00:33:23.070 --> 00:33:24.737
And on the weekends, I like to read a book

00:33:24.737 --> 00:33:27.510
with my phone nowhere in
sight, not on a device,

00:33:27.510 --> 00:33:30.330
that's what I do or I'll listen
to an audiobook sometimes

00:33:30.330 --> 00:33:31.320
while taking a walk,

00:33:31.320 --> 00:33:33.692
but really concentrating on
what I'm trying to learn,

00:33:33.692 --> 00:33:35.910
what I'm hearing and what I'm seeing.

00:33:35.910 --> 00:33:39.090
So again, a daily 90 minute
about is one to start with

00:33:39.090 --> 00:33:40.800
and I would say after
about four weeks of that,

00:33:40.800 --> 00:33:41.937
if you're able to stay concentrated

00:33:41.937 --> 00:33:43.440
and work through the agitation,

00:33:43.440 --> 00:33:46.590
then I would consider increasing
the number of focus bouts.

00:33:46.590 --> 00:33:47.643
Again, this is not to say

00:33:47.643 --> 00:33:49.410
that you should go to your teacher

00:33:49.410 --> 00:33:52.687
or your PhD advisor or your
parent or your friends and say,

00:33:52.687 --> 00:33:55.110
"Listen, I can't really
concentrate or think about anything

00:33:55.110 --> 00:33:56.730
for more than 90 minutes per day."

00:33:56.730 --> 00:33:57.570
That's not what I'm saying.

00:33:57.570 --> 00:33:58.890
These are deep focus bouts.

00:33:58.890 --> 00:34:00.660
These are bouts of work,

00:34:00.660 --> 00:34:03.300
or I should say mental
work or physical work

00:34:03.300 --> 00:34:06.150
where you're really forcing
yourself to focus and refocus,

00:34:06.150 --> 00:34:07.860
to sharpen the head of that arrowhead,

00:34:07.860 --> 00:34:09.930
to redirect it to what you're
trying to concentrate on,

00:34:09.930 --> 00:34:11.460
and it is indeed hard work.

00:34:11.460 --> 00:34:12.600
I would even think about it

00:34:12.600 --> 00:34:15.180
more or less like a workout of any kind.

