00:36:53.250 --> 00:36:55.920
I mentioned the topic of
nutrition a little bit ago,

00:36:55.920 --> 00:36:58.080
and of course, nutrition
is a complicated topic.

00:36:58.080 --> 00:36:59.430
In fact, one of the quickest ways

00:36:59.430 --> 00:37:01.530
to get yourself into a battle online

00:37:01.530 --> 00:37:03.900
is to say something
definitive about nutrition.

00:37:03.900 --> 00:37:06.330
I just want to clearly state
my stance about nutrition.

00:37:06.330 --> 00:37:07.920
I fully support and applaud

00:37:07.920 --> 00:37:09.720
those of you that are
vegans for whatever reason,

00:37:09.720 --> 00:37:11.910
those of you that are pure
carnivore for whatever reason,

00:37:11.910 --> 00:37:14.040
and those of you that are
omnivores for whatever reason.

00:37:14.040 --> 00:37:15.390
I happen to be an omnivore.

00:37:15.390 --> 00:37:17.250
My goal is always to eat high quality,

00:37:17.250 --> 00:37:19.140
minimally or non-processed foods

00:37:19.140 --> 00:37:20.400
and to eat things in moderation.

00:37:20.400 --> 00:37:21.960
So I do eat some meat

00:37:21.960 --> 00:37:24.720
from sustainable sources
or from organic sources,

00:37:24.720 --> 00:37:27.900
I eat some starches and I eat
vegetables and I eat fruits,

00:37:27.900 --> 00:37:29.220
I try not to eat sugars

00:37:29.220 --> 00:37:30.780
and I don't really like
highly processed foods

00:37:30.780 --> 00:37:31.800
at this point in my life.

00:37:31.800 --> 00:37:33.000
That's me, that's what I do,

00:37:33.000 --> 00:37:35.340
but I'm certainly not dictating
what people should eat.

00:37:35.340 --> 00:37:36.960
I know certain people are ketogenic,

00:37:36.960 --> 00:37:40.050
and I can say that for
people who achieve ketosis

00:37:40.050 --> 00:37:41.280
and can get into ketosis,

00:37:41.280 --> 00:37:44.820
yes indeed, there is a mental
state associated with ketosis

00:37:44.820 --> 00:37:46.770
that will allow your brain to function

00:37:46.770 --> 00:37:48.060
and to think really clearly

00:37:48.060 --> 00:37:49.860
that many people find very attractive

00:37:49.860 --> 00:37:52.140
and keep them going
back over and over again

00:37:52.140 --> 00:37:53.430
to a ketogenic diet.

00:37:53.430 --> 00:37:56.130
I'm somebody who for instance
has not been in ketosis

00:37:56.130 --> 00:37:57.030
many times in my life,

00:37:57.030 --> 00:37:58.680
at least not deliberately so,

00:37:58.680 --> 00:38:01.350
but I actually will ingest
liquid ketones from time to time

00:38:01.350 --> 00:38:03.690
because of the further
cognitive enhancement

00:38:03.690 --> 00:38:05.310
and physical enhancement that I experience

00:38:05.310 --> 00:38:08.730
on top of nutrition that does
include some carbohydrates.

00:38:08.730 --> 00:38:11.100
So there are a lot of different
ways to approach all this,

00:38:11.100 --> 00:38:12.810
whether or not you're a vegan, omnivore,

00:38:12.810 --> 00:38:16.171
vegetarian, carnivore, et
cetera, the point is this,

00:38:16.171 --> 00:38:17.760
your ability to focus,

00:38:17.760 --> 00:38:19.650
and in fact, your ability of neurons

00:38:19.650 --> 00:38:22.470
to encode specific information
in your environment,

00:38:22.470 --> 00:38:25.140
that is to represent what's
out there in the world,

00:38:25.140 --> 00:38:28.080
is actually related to
your blood glucose level.

00:38:28.080 --> 00:38:30.720
Now here I'm setting aside the discussion

00:38:30.720 --> 00:38:33.870
of ketosis and ketogenic
diets for the moment,

00:38:33.870 --> 00:38:34.890
but there's a beautiful study

00:38:34.890 --> 00:38:36.450
that was published in Neuron not long ago

00:38:36.450 --> 00:38:37.500
that showed that the tuning,

00:38:37.500 --> 00:38:40.350
that is the precision with
which neurons in the brain

00:38:40.350 --> 00:38:42.390
will represent things in our environment

00:38:42.390 --> 00:38:44.730
is actually much greater

00:38:44.730 --> 00:38:47.040
when there is sufficient
glucose in the brain.

00:38:47.040 --> 00:38:50.070
Translated into English, this
means that when we are fasted

00:38:50.070 --> 00:38:52.020
or when our blood glucose is very low,

00:38:52.020 --> 00:38:54.840
we aren't able to perceive
and think about things

00:38:54.840 --> 00:38:56.040
as clearly.

00:38:56.040 --> 00:38:57.720
Now there's a twist to this, however.

00:38:57.720 --> 00:38:59.820
Many people who practice
intermittent fasting,

00:38:59.820 --> 00:39:00.653
and I should say

00:39:00.653 --> 00:39:02.430
I practice a sort of pseudo
intermittent fasting,

00:39:02.430 --> 00:39:03.630
I generally eat my meals

00:39:03.630 --> 00:39:05.850
between the hours of 11:00 AM and 8:00 PM

00:39:05.850 --> 00:39:07.890
although sometimes there's
some wiggle around that,

00:39:07.890 --> 00:39:09.270
occasionally have an early breakfast,

00:39:09.270 --> 00:39:10.710
I'm not super rigid about it,

00:39:10.710 --> 00:39:12.030
but I know there are a number of people

00:39:12.030 --> 00:39:13.260
who are doing longer fasts

00:39:13.260 --> 00:39:15.108
or they're eating in a six hour window.

00:39:15.108 --> 00:39:17.850
We did an entire episode about fasting,

00:39:17.850 --> 00:39:19.980
you can again find that
at hubermanlab.com,

00:39:19.980 --> 00:39:21.750
we'll likely have Satchin Panda,

00:39:21.750 --> 00:39:24.810
who's an expert in intermittent
fasting, on the podcast.

00:39:24.810 --> 00:39:26.250
Intermittent fasting

00:39:26.250 --> 00:39:28.608
has a lot of different potential benefits.

00:39:28.608 --> 00:39:29.610
For some people,

00:39:29.610 --> 00:39:31.470
it's a convenient way to
restrict their calories.

00:39:31.470 --> 00:39:33.660
For other people, it's a
convenient way to avoid eating,

00:39:33.660 --> 00:39:36.300
that is it's easier to not eat
than to eat a small portion

00:39:36.300 --> 00:39:39.870
so they opt for intermittent
fasting and so on and so forth.

00:39:39.870 --> 00:39:41.730
But one of the things
that you hear very often

00:39:41.730 --> 00:39:43.440
is that some people like being fasted

00:39:43.440 --> 00:39:46.740
because they like the clarity
of mind that it provides.

00:39:46.740 --> 00:39:48.060
Here's the situation.

00:39:48.060 --> 00:39:50.010
Neurons, unless you're
in a ketogenic diet,

00:39:50.010 --> 00:39:53.130
really thrive on glucose,
they love glucose.

00:39:53.130 --> 00:39:54.690
And as I mentioned before,

00:39:54.690 --> 00:39:56.520
your ability to think and perceive things

00:39:56.520 --> 00:39:57.870
is actually enhanced

00:39:57.870 --> 00:40:00.060
by having sufficient
glucose in your bloodstream.

00:40:00.060 --> 00:40:02.460
So why would it be that
some people experience

00:40:02.460 --> 00:40:05.100
a heightened state of mental
clarity when they are fasted?

00:40:05.100 --> 00:40:06.572
I've certainly experienced that before.

00:40:06.572 --> 00:40:07.500
Well, I should say

00:40:07.500 --> 00:40:09.540
that provided you're well hydrated enough

00:40:09.540 --> 00:40:11.490
and you have enough
electrolytes in your system,

00:40:11.490 --> 00:40:14.130
what tends to happen is
that when you ingest food,

00:40:14.130 --> 00:40:15.660
there's a shift in your nervous system

00:40:15.660 --> 00:40:17.700
towards so-called parasympathetic mode,

00:40:17.700 --> 00:40:19.200
that is the more relaxed,

00:40:19.200 --> 00:40:20.790
you've probably heard
it as rest and digest,

00:40:20.790 --> 00:40:21.623
although it does other things,

00:40:21.623 --> 00:40:24.540
the more relaxed mode that can
indeed make us very sleepy.

00:40:24.540 --> 00:40:25.830
If we have too many carbohydrates,

00:40:25.830 --> 00:40:27.450
it actually can make us quite sleepy.

00:40:27.450 --> 00:40:29.730
However, if we have any food,

00:40:29.730 --> 00:40:31.140
if we have enough of it,

00:40:31.140 --> 00:40:32.760
that is if our gut is full,

00:40:32.760 --> 00:40:34.050
it diverts blood to our gut

00:40:34.050 --> 00:40:36.450
and we become sleepy and
we can't focus as well.

00:40:36.450 --> 00:40:38.400
So a lot of people really like fasting

00:40:38.400 --> 00:40:41.700
and the state of being fasted
for focus and concentration

00:40:41.700 --> 00:40:43.980
because they don't have as much

00:40:43.980 --> 00:40:45.930
of that parasympathetic activation,

00:40:45.930 --> 00:40:46.950
they're just not as sleepy.

00:40:46.950 --> 00:40:48.570
And in fact, under those conditions,

00:40:48.570 --> 00:40:51.330
half as much caffeine will
give you just as much lift

00:40:51.330 --> 00:40:53.490
as twice as much caffeine will give you

00:40:53.490 --> 00:40:54.720
on a full belly of pasta

00:40:54.720 --> 00:40:56.520
and that's just the way
that caffeine interacts

00:40:56.520 --> 00:40:57.360
with blood glucose.

00:40:57.360 --> 00:40:58.920
So what I'd like you to imagine

00:40:58.920 --> 00:41:02.310
is if you had a measure
of focus from zero to 10,

00:41:02.310 --> 00:41:03.420
these are arbitrary units,

00:41:03.420 --> 00:41:04.800
10 being maximally focused

00:41:04.800 --> 00:41:06.960
and zero being not focused at all,

00:41:06.960 --> 00:41:10.020
imagine a U-shaped function, right,

00:41:10.020 --> 00:41:11.850
where if you're very fasted,

00:41:11.850 --> 00:41:14.850
you're going to have high degree
of focus and concentration,

00:41:14.850 --> 00:41:17.340
but then if you ingest some
food and your belly is full,

00:41:17.340 --> 00:41:19.680
your focus and concentration is reduced.

00:41:19.680 --> 00:41:21.916
But having enough blood glucose

00:41:21.916 --> 00:41:24.360
and maybe even elevated blood glucose

00:41:24.360 --> 00:41:25.830
will increase cognitive function.

00:41:25.830 --> 00:41:27.060
So there are two ends of the spectrum.

00:41:27.060 --> 00:41:28.320
On one end of the spectrum,

00:41:28.320 --> 00:41:31.140
blood glucose is relatively
low and you're fasted

00:41:31.140 --> 00:41:34.680
and you can think and behave
in a very concentrated way

00:41:34.680 --> 00:41:36.300
and on the other end of the spectrum,

00:41:36.300 --> 00:41:38.160
you have a lot of blood
glucose, or I should say,

00:41:38.160 --> 00:41:39.150
sufficient blood glucose,

00:41:39.150 --> 00:41:41.460
you never want your blood
glucose to be too high,

00:41:41.460 --> 00:41:45.210
and that allows your neurons
to encode and perceive

00:41:45.210 --> 00:41:47.430
and basically allow you
to think really clearly.

00:41:47.430 --> 00:41:49.500
So you sort of have to
pick your condition.

00:41:49.500 --> 00:41:52.800
What do you want for your bouts
of focus and concentration?

00:41:52.800 --> 00:41:53.820
I actually do both.

00:41:53.820 --> 00:41:55.350
So what I do is, as I mentioned before,

00:41:55.350 --> 00:41:57.330
I eat my meal sometime around 11:00 AM,

00:41:57.330 --> 00:41:58.470
my first meal typically

00:41:58.470 --> 00:42:00.060
unless I'm very hungry when I wake up.

00:42:00.060 --> 00:42:04.600
And so I will do my workout
and one about of focused work.

00:42:04.600 --> 00:42:07.530
I always think of this as my
hard work early in the day

00:42:07.530 --> 00:42:08.940
and I do that fasted,

00:42:08.940 --> 00:42:11.220
I'll be consuming water with electrolytes,

00:42:11.220 --> 00:42:12.660
maybe LMNT or other electrolytes,

00:42:12.660 --> 00:42:15.960
maybe some caffeine as well
in the form of yerba mate

00:42:15.960 --> 00:42:20.340
or coffee, that's my first focus
about of 90 minutes or less,

00:42:20.340 --> 00:42:22.860
that is essentially done
fasted and then I'll eat

00:42:22.860 --> 00:42:24.720
and then I do notice after I eat,

00:42:24.720 --> 00:42:26.880
I actually have a diminished
capacity to focus.

00:42:26.880 --> 00:42:28.230
But then again in the afternoon,

00:42:28.230 --> 00:42:30.180
I will do another 90
minute about of focus,

00:42:30.180 --> 00:42:31.710
and I'll talk about
some of the tools I use

00:42:31.710 --> 00:42:34.110
to make sure that that
about of focus is optimal

00:42:34.110 --> 00:42:37.440
for getting the most amount
of focused work done,

00:42:37.440 --> 00:42:39.330
whether or not it's mental
work or physical work,

00:42:39.330 --> 00:42:41.337
although I tend to do my
physical work early in the day

00:42:41.337 --> 00:42:44.550
and my mental work both
early and late in the day.

00:42:44.550 --> 00:42:48.330
So to make this very simple
or as simple as I can for you

00:42:48.330 --> 00:42:52.050
being fasted is great for
focus and concentration

00:42:52.050 --> 00:42:55.140
provided you're not thinking
about food the entire time

00:42:55.140 --> 00:42:58.040
and being fed is terrific
for focus and concentration,

00:42:58.040 --> 00:43:00.540
it actually can improve neuronal function

00:43:00.540 --> 00:43:02.970
provided that you didn't
eat too much food.

00:43:02.970 --> 00:43:04.980
So one way to manage this

00:43:04.980 --> 00:43:06.240
is if you're going to have a lunch,

00:43:06.240 --> 00:43:08.370
to make sure that you don't
stuff yourself at lunch,

00:43:08.370 --> 00:43:11.730
that you're not overeating
and to not get quite so full

00:43:11.730 --> 00:43:13.680
that you put your nervous system

00:43:13.680 --> 00:43:15.180
into this parasympathetic mode

00:43:15.180 --> 00:43:17.250
and make it hard to
focus in the afternoon.

00:43:17.250 --> 00:43:18.600
I know a lot of people experience a dip

00:43:18.600 --> 00:43:20.340
or even a crash in energy in the afternoon

00:43:20.340 --> 00:43:21.900
that make it really hard to focus.

00:43:21.900 --> 00:43:23.360
For that reason, I'll just remind people

00:43:23.360 --> 00:43:25.282
of a tool I've talked
about many times before,

00:43:25.282 --> 00:43:26.760
which is based on the biology

00:43:26.760 --> 00:43:28.590
of adenosine and caffeine, et cetera,

00:43:28.590 --> 00:43:31.110
which is to delay your
first caffeine intake

00:43:31.110 --> 00:43:33.660
to 90 to 120 minutes after waking up.

00:43:33.660 --> 00:43:35.386
I know that can be painful
for certain people.

00:43:35.386 --> 00:43:36.870
I violate that rule,

00:43:36.870 --> 00:43:38.790
when I'm working out very
early in the morning.

00:43:38.790 --> 00:43:40.922
I'll drink my caffeine before my workout

00:43:40.922 --> 00:43:45.210
which often occurs within
30 to 60 minutes of waking.

00:43:45.210 --> 00:43:47.940
But in general, unless I'm
working out very early,

00:43:47.940 --> 00:43:52.500
I will ingest my caffeine 90
to 120 minutes after I wake up.

00:43:52.500 --> 00:43:53.880
So again, I want to emphasize

00:43:53.880 --> 00:43:56.160
that if you hear somebody out there say

00:43:56.160 --> 00:43:59.640
being fasted is optimal for
focus and concentration.

00:43:59.640 --> 00:44:02.220
Well, that is true in one context

00:44:02.220 --> 00:44:04.800
and perhaps ideal for a
certain part of the day.

00:44:04.800 --> 00:44:07.980
And other people will say,
no, neurons run on glucose,

00:44:07.980 --> 00:44:09.870
you need glucose in your bloodstream

00:44:09.870 --> 00:44:12.390
in order to get those neurons to be tuned,

00:44:12.390 --> 00:44:14.520
that is to respond with
electrical activity

00:44:14.520 --> 00:44:16.950
in the optimal way when
you're reading something

00:44:16.950 --> 00:44:18.210
or when you're trying to perform exercise.

00:44:18.210 --> 00:44:19.290
Well, that's also true.

00:44:19.290 --> 00:44:21.330
And of course, you can incorporate both.

00:44:21.330 --> 00:44:23.790
I, in fact as I just described,

00:44:23.790 --> 00:44:27.090
incorporate both fasted
states and fed states

00:44:27.090 --> 00:44:30.063
in order to optimize my
concentration and focus.

00:44:30.930 --> 00:44:33.210
And as a brief note about ketosis,

00:44:33.210 --> 00:44:34.770
for those of you that actually managed

00:44:34.770 --> 00:44:36.210
to transition into ketosis

00:44:36.210 --> 00:44:38.640
and are maintaining a ketogenic state,

00:44:38.640 --> 00:44:40.680
that, as I mentioned earlier,

00:44:40.680 --> 00:44:44.850
can enhance brain function,
concentration and focus

00:44:44.850 --> 00:44:47.460
because of the way in
which ketones can be used

00:44:47.460 --> 00:44:50.850
as a so-called optimal fuel for neurons.

00:44:50.850 --> 00:44:54.210
The ketogenic diet was
originally designed, if you will,

00:44:54.210 --> 00:44:55.470
for epilepsy,

00:44:55.470 --> 00:44:57.000
it has a whole relationship to epilepsy

00:44:57.000 --> 00:44:58.710
and controlling epileptic seizures,

00:44:58.710 --> 00:45:00.990
and it can in fact allow people

00:45:00.990 --> 00:45:03.330
to achieve focus,
concentrated brain states.

00:45:03.330 --> 00:45:05.370
So in the future, I'll do
an episode about ketosis

00:45:05.370 --> 00:45:07.590
and be sure to circle back
on how to optimize ketosis

00:45:07.590 --> 00:45:08.790
for focus and concentration.

00:45:08.790 --> 00:45:09.780
Although I have to believe

00:45:09.780 --> 00:45:11.130
that most of the people listening to this

00:45:11.130 --> 00:45:14.040
are probably not in ketosis
or following a ketogenic diet

00:45:14.040 --> 00:45:15.210
so that's why I mainly focused

00:45:15.210 --> 00:45:18.870
on fasted states and fed states.

00:45:18.870 --> 00:45:20.730
And just to make sure that I'm thorough,

00:45:20.730 --> 00:45:22.050
a fasted state to me

00:45:22.050 --> 00:45:25.230
would be a state in which you
haven't ingested any calories,

00:45:25.230 --> 00:45:27.090
but may have ingested caffeine

00:45:27.090 --> 00:45:29.160
or maybe even a small amount
of artificial sweetener

00:45:29.160 --> 00:45:30.510
or something like that,

00:45:30.510 --> 00:45:31.950
but really haven't ingested

00:45:31.950 --> 00:45:33.690
any significant number of calories

00:45:33.690 --> 00:45:36.870
in the previous four to
eight or maybe even 12 hours.

00:45:36.870 --> 00:45:38.700
And again, there's
tremendous variation here,

00:45:38.700 --> 00:45:40.410
depending on how long people have fasted,

00:45:40.410 --> 00:45:41.670
whether or not we're
talking about the state

00:45:41.670 --> 00:45:43.410
right after people wake up, et cetera.

00:45:43.410 --> 00:45:45.150
Again, if you're interested
in intermittent fasting,

00:45:45.150 --> 00:45:46.530
both for the sake of mental

00:45:46.530 --> 00:45:48.660
and physical health and performance,

00:45:48.660 --> 00:45:51.240
check out our episode on
fasting at hubermanlab.com.

00:45:51.240 --> 00:45:53.160
I also want to touch back on this idea

00:45:53.160 --> 00:45:55.482
of which foods can increase focus.

00:45:55.482 --> 00:45:58.410
You know, in the episode
on ADHD that I did,

00:45:58.410 --> 00:46:00.390
I touched on this quite a bit

00:46:00.390 --> 00:46:02.340
as it relates to elimination diets.

00:46:02.340 --> 00:46:04.200
There's a whole industry

00:46:04.200 --> 00:46:06.510
and a ton of interest for obvious reasons

00:46:06.510 --> 00:46:08.970
into what sorts of things kids and adults

00:46:08.970 --> 00:46:09.900
should and shouldn't eat

00:46:09.900 --> 00:46:12.030
in order to reduce symptoms of ADHD.

00:46:12.030 --> 00:46:15.660
I think that the sum total of
those data point to the fact

00:46:15.660 --> 00:46:17.310
that reducing simple sugar intake

00:46:17.310 --> 00:46:19.320
and certainly highly processed foods,

00:46:19.320 --> 00:46:22.020
so ice cream, candy, chips, et cetera,

00:46:22.020 --> 00:46:22.853
those sorts of things,

00:46:22.853 --> 00:46:25.170
really does seem to
improve symptoms of ADHD

00:46:25.170 --> 00:46:26.940
in both children and adults.

00:46:26.940 --> 00:46:28.950
But once you move past
that and you start to say,

00:46:28.950 --> 00:46:31.830
well, which foods can improve
concentration and focus?

00:46:31.830 --> 00:46:35.400
Well, foods that for instance
include a lot of tyrosine,

00:46:35.400 --> 00:46:36.870
which is a precursor to dopamine,

00:46:36.870 --> 00:46:40.170
and now you know why dopamine
is important in this context,

00:46:40.170 --> 00:46:42.720
are certainly going to increase
concentration and focus.

00:46:42.720 --> 00:46:44.790
So things like Parmesan cheese,

00:46:44.790 --> 00:46:46.020
certain meats, certain nuts,

00:46:46.020 --> 00:46:47.460
you can look up which foods

00:46:47.460 --> 00:46:48.930
contain high amounts of tyrosine.

00:46:48.930 --> 00:46:50.700
There are also some fruits and vegetables

00:46:50.700 --> 00:46:52.620
that include high amounts of tyrosine,

00:46:52.620 --> 00:46:55.260
but to be quite direct,

00:46:55.260 --> 00:46:59.130
it doesn't matter whether or
not you're ingesting foods

00:46:59.130 --> 00:47:01.740
that are rich in the precursor amino acids

00:47:01.740 --> 00:47:04.020
to dopamine, acetylcholine, et cetera,

00:47:04.020 --> 00:47:07.020
if you are consuming large
amounts of those foods.

00:47:07.020 --> 00:47:09.960
That is one can look and see for instance,

00:47:09.960 --> 00:47:13.470
that a steak includes
a lot of the precursors

00:47:13.470 --> 00:47:14.730
to acetylcholine.

00:47:14.730 --> 00:47:17.640
It has amino acid precursors
to dopamine as well

00:47:17.640 --> 00:47:19.530
and there are other foods
that will do that as well,

00:47:19.530 --> 00:47:22.560
but if I were to ingest
say two ribeye steaks,

00:47:22.560 --> 00:47:24.030
that's a lot of meat

00:47:24.030 --> 00:47:26.850
and it will direct a
lot of blood to my gut

00:47:26.850 --> 00:47:29.130
and it will cause me to be sleepy

00:47:29.130 --> 00:47:31.140
and that will create challenges of me

00:47:31.140 --> 00:47:33.990
being able to achieve states
of focus and concentration.

00:47:33.990 --> 00:47:36.570
So the simple way to put
this is if you eat too much

00:47:36.570 --> 00:47:38.670
or you eat a very large volume of food,

00:47:38.670 --> 00:47:42.690
you are going to diminish
your focus and concentration.

00:47:42.690 --> 00:47:44.040
The key is to eat enough

00:47:44.040 --> 00:47:46.560
that you're nourished for
the certain activities,

00:47:46.560 --> 00:47:48.930
mental and physical that
you need to perform,

00:47:48.930 --> 00:47:50.520
but if you're eating large meals,

00:47:50.520 --> 00:47:53.700
you are going to diminish
your concentration and focus

00:47:53.700 --> 00:47:54.533
period.

00:47:54.533 --> 00:47:57.150
I know many people are curious
as to whether or not caffeine

00:47:57.150 --> 00:48:01.710
can improve focus and
concentration, and indeed it can.

00:48:01.710 --> 00:48:04.980
There is an immense amount
of data supporting the idea

00:48:04.980 --> 00:48:05.814
that caffeine,

00:48:05.814 --> 00:48:08.250
provided its consumed in
the appropriate dosages,

00:48:08.250 --> 00:48:11.160
can improve mental performance
and physical performance,

00:48:11.160 --> 00:48:12.960
and it largely does that

00:48:12.960 --> 00:48:16.388
through improvements in
focus and concentration.

00:48:16.388 --> 00:48:18.570
The dosage of caffeine of course,

00:48:18.570 --> 00:48:21.300
is going to depend on how
caffeine adapted you are,

00:48:21.300 --> 00:48:23.490
how much caffeine tolerance you have.

00:48:23.490 --> 00:48:25.795
And that is going to vary tremendously

00:48:25.795 --> 00:48:28.920
depending on whether or not
you ingest that caffeine

00:48:28.920 --> 00:48:31.410
with or without food,
as I mentioned earlier.

00:48:31.410 --> 00:48:33.600
But there is a kind of general range

00:48:33.600 --> 00:48:35.610
in which we can talk about caffeine

00:48:35.610 --> 00:48:37.830
as being useful for
focus and concentration.

00:48:37.830 --> 00:48:39.690
And the range is basically

00:48:39.690 --> 00:48:42.330
from 100 milligrams to 400 milligrams.

00:48:42.330 --> 00:48:44.040
I want to caution everybody out there,

00:48:44.040 --> 00:48:46.470
if you're somebody who
suffers from anxiety

00:48:46.470 --> 00:48:47.700
or panic attacks,

00:48:47.700 --> 00:48:49.380
and you're not used to ingesting caffeine

00:48:49.380 --> 00:48:51.920
and you run out and ingest
400 milligrams of caffeine

00:48:51.920 --> 00:48:53.970
in the form of espresso or yerba mate

00:48:53.970 --> 00:48:56.400
or an energy drink or in pill form,

00:48:56.400 --> 00:48:58.410
that is going to be very
uncomfortable for you,

00:48:58.410 --> 00:48:59.880
you're going to be sweating profusely,

00:48:59.880 --> 00:49:01.140
your heart rate is going to increase,

00:49:01.140 --> 00:49:04.080
you're going to be quite panicked in fact

00:49:04.080 --> 00:49:05.460
or at least anxious.

00:49:05.460 --> 00:49:09.210
So be cautious with your
use and adopting of caffeine

00:49:09.210 --> 00:49:11.460
if you're not already caffeine adapted,

00:49:11.460 --> 00:49:12.780
but most people do quite well

00:49:12.780 --> 00:49:15.450
to ingest 100 to 200
milligrams of caffeine

00:49:15.450 --> 00:49:17.640
prior to doing some focused work.

00:49:17.640 --> 00:49:20.310
And again, I recommend
delaying your caffeine intake

00:49:20.310 --> 00:49:22.470
to 90 to 120 minutes after waking,

00:49:22.470 --> 00:49:24.330
unless you're using that caffeine

00:49:24.330 --> 00:49:28.170
to really jolt your
system before a workout.

00:49:28.170 --> 00:49:31.020
Caffeine can of course be
ingested in various forms,

00:49:31.020 --> 00:49:32.040
even pill form,

00:49:32.040 --> 00:49:33.870
but most people ingest
it in the form of coffee

00:49:33.870 --> 00:49:36.480
or my particular favorite
way to ingest caffeine

00:49:36.480 --> 00:49:37.890
is yerba mate.

00:49:37.890 --> 00:49:40.050
It is important and I should note

00:49:40.050 --> 00:49:42.360
that you should actively avoid

00:49:42.360 --> 00:49:44.010
the smoked versions of yerba mate

00:49:44.010 --> 00:49:45.780
as they contain a lot of carcinogenic,

00:49:45.780 --> 00:49:47.400
cancer promoting compounds.

00:49:47.400 --> 00:49:49.887
There's some great yerba
mate brands out there.

00:49:49.887 --> 00:49:52.470
The most cost effective way to consume it

00:49:52.470 --> 00:49:53.850
would be to use the loose leaf tea

00:49:53.850 --> 00:49:55.641
and to pour water over that.

00:49:55.641 --> 00:49:57.660
There's one particular brand that I like,

00:49:57.660 --> 00:50:00.090
I don't have any affiliation
to them whatsoever,

00:50:00.090 --> 00:50:02.040
but I've been using it
for years, it's Anna Park.

00:50:02.040 --> 00:50:05.490
It's an organic brand that is
sold, I buy mine on Amazon,

00:50:05.490 --> 00:50:07.380
you can find it elsewhere
on the internet as well.

00:50:07.380 --> 00:50:09.030
Again, I don't have any
affiliation to them,

00:50:09.030 --> 00:50:10.710
it's just very cost effective, very clean,

00:50:10.710 --> 00:50:12.570
it doesn't have the smoked flavor,

00:50:12.570 --> 00:50:14.490
at least the one that I buy
is not the smoked variety

00:50:14.490 --> 00:50:16.860
so none of the carcinogenic
compounds are in there

00:50:16.860 --> 00:50:20.190
at least that I'm aware of
and I like the way it tastes

00:50:20.190 --> 00:50:23.530
and it provides a very
even lift and stimulant

00:50:23.530 --> 00:50:25.620
that I think certainly works for me

00:50:25.620 --> 00:50:26.597
and that a number of people I know

00:50:26.597 --> 00:50:29.430
that I have suggested to also enjoy.

00:50:29.430 --> 00:50:34.320
Yerba mate or caffeine also
have other additional benefits,

00:50:34.320 --> 00:50:38.010
in particular, the caffeine
in yerba mate and coffee

00:50:38.010 --> 00:50:39.120
and other sources of caffeine

00:50:39.120 --> 00:50:42.300
are known to increase
the density and efficacy,

00:50:42.300 --> 00:50:46.710
that is the number and the
function of dopamine receptors,

00:50:46.710 --> 00:50:49.350
and this has been shown
in humans several times.

00:50:49.350 --> 00:50:51.300
So by ingesting caffeine pretty regularly,

00:50:51.300 --> 00:50:53.610
you're actually increasing
the ability of dopamine

00:50:53.610 --> 00:50:57.930
to have this effect of
increasing motivation and drive.

00:50:57.930 --> 00:51:00.390
I tend to ingest caffeine
only early in the day,

00:51:00.390 --> 00:51:01.980
I tend to cut off my caffeine intake

00:51:01.980 --> 00:51:03.660
somewhere around one or 2:00 PM

00:51:03.660 --> 00:51:05.820
to ensure that I can get
into a good night's sleep,

00:51:05.820 --> 00:51:07.230
but I realize that there
are people out there

00:51:07.230 --> 00:51:08.130
that ingest caffeine

00:51:08.130 --> 00:51:09.930
as late as two or three in the afternoon

00:51:09.930 --> 00:51:11.340
and can still sleep fine.

00:51:11.340 --> 00:51:12.570
I will caution those of you

00:51:12.570 --> 00:51:13.920
that think that you can drink caffeine

00:51:13.920 --> 00:51:16.063
in the evening or nighttime
and still fall asleep.

00:51:16.063 --> 00:51:18.540
All of the research points to the fact

00:51:18.540 --> 00:51:20.010
that the architecture of your sleep

00:51:20.010 --> 00:51:21.660
and the depth of your sleep is disrupted.

00:51:21.660 --> 00:51:24.330
Even if you're able to
fall and stay asleep,

00:51:24.330 --> 00:51:26.010
the sleep you're getting
is simply not as good

00:51:26.010 --> 00:51:26.880
as the sleep you would get

00:51:26.880 --> 00:51:29.191
if you were to shut off
your caffeine intake

00:51:29.191 --> 00:51:31.230
at least eight hours before bedtime,

00:51:31.230 --> 00:51:33.960
and ideally more like 10 or
even 12 hours before bedtime.

00:51:33.960 --> 00:51:36.780
But of course, there are
practical constraints as well.

00:51:36.780 --> 00:51:41.010
Okay so caffeine is
increasing dopamine's function

00:51:41.010 --> 00:51:44.939
by changing the number and
efficacy of dopamine receptors,

00:51:44.939 --> 00:51:47.130
but of course, it also
increases our wakefulness,

00:51:47.130 --> 00:51:48.270
our alertness,

00:51:48.270 --> 00:51:51.210
and that is largely through
the neurochemical systems

00:51:51.210 --> 00:51:52.530
related to adenosine,

00:51:52.530 --> 00:51:54.660
which is a molecule that
builds up in our brain and body

00:51:54.660 --> 00:51:55.710
the longer we are awake,

00:51:55.710 --> 00:51:58.560
it's part of the sleepiness
system if you will,

00:51:58.560 --> 00:52:00.780
it makes us feel fatigued or tired,

00:52:00.780 --> 00:52:05.160
and caffeine also operates
on the epinephrine,

00:52:05.160 --> 00:52:06.480
the adrenaline system.

00:52:06.480 --> 00:52:08.070
In fact, if we ingest too much caffeine,

00:52:08.070 --> 00:52:09.840
we'll sometimes get the jitters.

00:52:09.840 --> 00:52:13.410
Those jitters are really the
sympathetic, as it's called,

00:52:13.410 --> 00:52:15.750
nervous system's bias toward movement,

00:52:15.750 --> 00:52:19.470
and our pupils will dilate,
they actually get broader.

00:52:19.470 --> 00:52:23.880
Now somewhat paradoxically,
when our pupils get bigger,

00:52:23.880 --> 00:52:25.980
the pupils of our eyes that is,

00:52:25.980 --> 00:52:27.840
our visual world actually narrows,

00:52:27.840 --> 00:52:29.370
it becomes more tunnel-like.

00:52:29.370 --> 00:52:30.300
A lot of people don't realize this.

00:52:30.300 --> 00:52:32.480
When our pupils are really
small, that means we are relaxed.

00:52:32.480 --> 00:52:33.360
So if you ever see someone

00:52:33.360 --> 00:52:35.940
with really tiny or pin size pupils,

00:52:35.940 --> 00:52:36.840
they're very relaxed.

00:52:36.840 --> 00:52:39.270
If their pupils are very
big, they're very dilated,

00:52:39.270 --> 00:52:42.000
well then they are very amped up,

00:52:42.000 --> 00:52:44.070
they are very, very alert.

00:52:44.070 --> 00:52:47.400
Caffeine increases alertness
by increasing epinephrine,

00:52:47.400 --> 00:52:50.907
adrenaline release both in
the brain and within the body.

00:52:50.907 --> 00:52:53.190
And so that's another
way that it facilitates

00:52:53.190 --> 00:52:55.244
focus and concentration.

00:52:55.244 --> 00:52:58.110
Now there are other ways
to increase epinephrine

00:52:58.110 --> 00:53:00.990
in the brain and body besides
caffeine or other stimulants,

00:53:00.990 --> 00:53:02.880
and in fact, that has been studied.

00:53:02.880 --> 00:53:04.050
There's an excellent study

00:53:04.050 --> 00:53:08.100
that was carried out not that
long ago on how stress itself

00:53:08.100 --> 00:53:11.190
can increase our ability
to focus and concentrate,

00:53:11.190 --> 00:53:12.690
that's right how stress itself

00:53:12.690 --> 00:53:14.580
can increase focus and concentration.

00:53:14.580 --> 00:53:15.870
You know, most people think of stress

00:53:15.870 --> 00:53:18.060
as impairing our ability to focus,

00:53:18.060 --> 00:53:19.290
but that's actually not true.

00:53:19.290 --> 00:53:22.230
When we are stressed, it
involves the deployment,

00:53:22.230 --> 00:53:24.630
the release of adrenaline, epinephrine,

00:53:24.630 --> 00:53:27.930
and that that adrenaline both
changes our visual field,

00:53:27.930 --> 00:53:29.700
in other words, it narrows our vision

00:53:29.700 --> 00:53:32.100
to a more tunnel-like focus,

00:53:32.100 --> 00:53:33.990
that is it makes the arrow

00:53:33.990 --> 00:53:37.410
in our metaphor of the arrow more sharp,

00:53:37.410 --> 00:53:39.927
and it improves our concentration.

00:53:39.927 --> 00:53:42.300
And this makes sense given
what we know about stress.

00:53:42.300 --> 00:53:43.652
When we're stressed,

00:53:43.652 --> 00:53:44.485
we tend to be stressed
about a specific thing,

00:53:44.485 --> 00:53:46.800
we start anticipating
or wondering or thinking

00:53:46.800 --> 00:53:47.850
about what's going to happen next?

00:53:47.850 --> 00:53:48.810
What led up to this?

00:53:48.810 --> 00:53:49.920
How is this going to impact me?

00:53:49.920 --> 00:53:51.360
How do I feel right now?

00:53:51.360 --> 00:53:53.160
It really narrows the context

00:53:53.160 --> 00:53:55.140
of our thinking and our behavior.

00:53:55.140 --> 00:53:57.120
So one of my favorite studies
that really illustrates

00:53:57.120 --> 00:54:00.450
how stress can improve
concentration and performance

00:54:00.450 --> 00:54:02.067
is one that was published
not that long ago

00:54:02.067 --> 00:54:04.560
and I will provide a link
to this in the show notes.

00:54:04.560 --> 00:54:05.880
It's a paper published

00:54:05.880 --> 00:54:08.820
in the "Journal of Experimental
Psychology" in 2020.

00:54:08.820 --> 00:54:11.017
The title of the paper
is, not surprisingly,

00:54:11.017 --> 00:54:14.070
"Acute stress improves
concentration performance",

00:54:14.070 --> 00:54:18.300
first author DeGroote, d-e-g-r-o-o-t-e.

00:54:18.300 --> 00:54:21.240
And this study involved
taking a number of subjects

00:54:21.240 --> 00:54:23.520
and stressing them out or not

00:54:23.520 --> 00:54:27.000
prior to a cognitive
or concentration task.

00:54:27.000 --> 00:54:28.650
And there are a lot of data in this paper,

00:54:28.650 --> 00:54:31.740
but I'm just going to hone in
on one specific set of data.

00:54:31.740 --> 00:54:33.270
And I should mention as I go there,

00:54:33.270 --> 00:54:35.820
that they measured things like
cortisol, a stress hormone,

00:54:35.820 --> 00:54:38.850
they measured anxiety, it
was a quite thorough study.

00:54:38.850 --> 00:54:42.000
And what they found was that
concentration performance

00:54:42.000 --> 00:54:45.330
improved manyfold, I should say, from,

00:54:45.330 --> 00:54:47.070
there was a greater than doubling

00:54:47.070 --> 00:54:50.460
of concentration and
performance in the stress group.

00:54:50.460 --> 00:54:52.620
And stress in this context was provided

00:54:52.620 --> 00:54:55.200
using a standard way of inducing stress.

00:54:55.200 --> 00:54:56.033
What they basically do

00:54:56.033 --> 00:54:58.050
is they bring subjects into the laboratory

00:54:58.050 --> 00:55:01.980
and they have to either do
something fairly mundane

00:55:01.980 --> 00:55:03.150
in the control group

00:55:03.150 --> 00:55:06.390
or they have to do a
simulated job interview,

00:55:06.390 --> 00:55:07.560
an arithmetic task,

00:55:07.560 --> 00:55:09.360
and they're being evaluated
as they're doing this.

00:55:09.360 --> 00:55:12.420
So this isn't intense psychosocial stress.

00:55:12.420 --> 00:55:14.160
They're not watching anything disturbing,

00:55:14.160 --> 00:55:15.990
they're not being traumatized
in any kind of way.

00:55:15.990 --> 00:55:18.390
This is fairly low levels of stress

00:55:18.390 --> 00:55:20.670
that raised their levels of epinephrine,

00:55:20.670 --> 00:55:22.560
and we know this from this study,

00:55:22.560 --> 00:55:25.230
and their levels of cortisol,
another stress hormone,

00:55:25.230 --> 00:55:28.230
modestly within their brain and blood.

00:55:28.230 --> 00:55:31.350
But that even modest increase
in these stress hormones

00:55:31.350 --> 00:55:34.590
and their reported
psychological levels of stress

00:55:34.590 --> 00:55:37.186
really enhanced their
focus and concentration.

00:55:37.186 --> 00:55:40.650
This may come as surprising
'cause like many people think,

00:55:40.650 --> 00:55:42.810
gosh, stress really diminishes
cognitive performance,

00:55:42.810 --> 00:55:44.070
but that's absolutely wrong,

00:55:44.070 --> 00:55:46.470
stress improves cognitive performance.

00:55:46.470 --> 00:55:49.350
Now of course there are other
ways to increase stress levels

00:55:49.350 --> 00:55:50.580
and to do that in healthy ways

00:55:50.580 --> 00:55:52.740
to improve concentration and performance

00:55:52.740 --> 00:55:54.840
and one of the best ways to do that

00:55:54.840 --> 00:55:58.050
because it's so sure fire
and it's generally safe

00:55:58.050 --> 00:56:01.680
provided you do it safely
is deliberate cold exposure.

00:56:01.680 --> 00:56:04.410
This is something I've talked
about on the podcast before,

00:56:04.410 --> 00:56:06.270
but deliberate cold
exposure can be achieved

00:56:06.270 --> 00:56:09.330
by getting into a cold shower
for one to five minutes.

00:56:09.330 --> 00:56:10.200
If you're not used to it,

00:56:10.200 --> 00:56:11.970
you probably want to
start with one minute,

00:56:11.970 --> 00:56:13.710
or you can get into an ice bath,

00:56:13.710 --> 00:56:15.030
and nowadays there are
a number of different

00:56:15.030 --> 00:56:17.160
commercial sources of
circulating cold water

00:56:17.160 --> 00:56:19.830
or if you have access
to a body of cold water

00:56:19.830 --> 00:56:22.260
like a lake or a pool or an ocean.

00:56:22.260 --> 00:56:26.310
We know that getting into
cold water or under cold water

00:56:26.310 --> 00:56:30.210
greatly increases epinephrine
levels and dopamine levels

00:56:30.210 --> 00:56:31.320
in the brain and blood.

00:56:31.320 --> 00:56:32.910
There's a beautiful
study that was published

00:56:32.910 --> 00:56:35.010
in the European Journal of Physiology

00:56:35.010 --> 00:56:38.370
that showed that the increases
in dopamine are massive,

00:56:38.370 --> 00:56:39.960
near doubling or more

00:56:39.960 --> 00:56:42.960
of dopamine levels that are
very long lasting for hours,

00:56:42.960 --> 00:56:46.110
and epinephrine, and
indeed cortisol levels

00:56:46.110 --> 00:56:47.700
are also increased,

00:56:47.700 --> 00:56:49.830
and in ways that support

00:56:49.830 --> 00:56:53.607
not just immune system
function because they do that,

00:56:53.607 --> 00:56:55.350
and mood because it does that,

00:56:55.350 --> 00:56:57.990
but they can really improve
concentration and focus.

00:56:57.990 --> 00:57:01.200
I touched on this a little
bit in an episode about memory

00:57:01.200 --> 00:57:03.960
that there's an age old practice

00:57:03.960 --> 00:57:06.030
really dating back to medieval times

00:57:06.030 --> 00:57:08.550
of putting people into cold water

00:57:08.550 --> 00:57:11.490
right after they learn
something in order to spike,

00:57:11.490 --> 00:57:12.780
to increase their epinephrine

00:57:12.780 --> 00:57:14.820
as a way to consolidate those memories.

00:57:14.820 --> 00:57:16.380
For the sake of today's discussion,

00:57:16.380 --> 00:57:17.400
if you are interested

00:57:17.400 --> 00:57:20.190
in ways to improve
focus and concentration,

00:57:20.190 --> 00:57:23.040
you need to increase your epinephrine,

00:57:23.040 --> 00:57:24.930
your adrenaline levels.

00:57:24.930 --> 00:57:26.373
Cold water exposure

00:57:26.373 --> 00:57:29.538
is one of the most
efficient ways to do that.

00:57:29.538 --> 00:57:31.620
This is not a biohack.

00:57:31.620 --> 00:57:33.120
I don't like the word hack,

00:57:33.120 --> 00:57:34.380
I know it's commonly used,

00:57:34.380 --> 00:57:36.720
but a hack is something
where you're using one thing

00:57:36.720 --> 00:57:38.880
for a different purpose than
it was originally intended for

00:57:38.880 --> 00:57:39.960
and here, I'm not referring

00:57:39.960 --> 00:57:41.040
to the shower or the cold bath,

00:57:41.040 --> 00:57:42.510
I'm referring to epinephrine.

00:57:42.510 --> 00:57:43.860
Epinephrine is a neurochemical

00:57:43.860 --> 00:57:47.520
that will place your vision
into more of a tunnel mode,

00:57:47.520 --> 00:57:48.540
which will allow you to focus

00:57:48.540 --> 00:57:51.480
on cognitive work or physical
work in a more specific way,

00:57:51.480 --> 00:57:53.070
you're not going to be as distractable,

00:57:53.070 --> 00:57:55.020
and it's very easy to achieve

00:57:55.020 --> 00:57:57.966
by getting into a cold shower
or a cold body of water

00:57:57.966 --> 00:58:00.030
for a brief period of time.

00:58:00.030 --> 00:58:04.770
People always ask how long to
get under or into cold water

00:58:04.770 --> 00:58:05.940
and how cold to make it.

00:58:05.940 --> 00:58:07.890
Here's the thing, it should
be uncomfortably cold,

00:58:07.890 --> 00:58:10.860
but safe to stay in for
one to five minutes.

00:58:10.860 --> 00:58:13.050
Okay so uncomfortably cold,
you really want to get out,

00:58:13.050 --> 00:58:13.883
but safe to stay in,

00:58:13.883 --> 00:58:15.900
not so cold that it's going
to give you a heart attack

00:58:15.900 --> 00:58:17.670
and not so warm that it's comfortable

00:58:17.670 --> 00:58:20.070
that it doesn't create
that adrenaline release.

00:58:20.070 --> 00:58:21.240
Cold water exposure,

00:58:21.240 --> 00:58:22.500
I should say deliberate, cold water

00:58:22.500 --> 00:58:24.671
or non deliberate cold water exposure

00:58:24.671 --> 00:58:28.050
reliably increases epinephrine levels.

00:58:28.050 --> 00:58:31.230
It is incredibly useful
as a tool for this,

00:58:31.230 --> 00:58:35.850
and it is in fact, zero cost
or even negative zero cost.

00:58:35.850 --> 00:58:37.140
How could it be negative zero cost?

00:58:37.140 --> 00:58:39.210
Well, you can certainly
save on your heating bill

00:58:39.210 --> 00:58:41.610
by taking a cold shower so that's one way.

00:58:41.610 --> 00:58:44.160
And for those of you that
have access to devices

00:58:44.160 --> 00:58:46.140
or locations where you
can get into cold water,

00:58:46.140 --> 00:58:47.130
you can submerge,

00:58:47.130 --> 00:58:48.450
well then that can work.

00:58:48.450 --> 00:58:50.848
For those of you that don't,
maybe you take a cold bath,

00:58:50.848 --> 00:58:53.190
you get in up to your neck,
that's going to be most efficient.

00:58:53.190 --> 00:58:54.660
For those of you that can't do that,

00:58:54.660 --> 00:58:55.740
you'll get under a cold shower.

00:58:55.740 --> 00:58:59.370
Again, it should be comfortably cold

00:58:59.370 --> 00:59:00.810
to the point where you want to get out,

00:59:00.810 --> 00:59:04.110
but that you can safely stay
in for one to five minutes.

00:59:04.110 --> 00:59:06.030
How long should you do
it before a workout?

00:59:06.030 --> 00:59:08.640
Well, if you get into really cold water,

00:59:08.640 --> 00:59:09.600
it's uncomfortably cold

00:59:09.600 --> 00:59:11.010
and get out after about three minutes,

00:59:11.010 --> 00:59:13.990
you're probably good to go,
dry off and get to work.

00:59:13.990 --> 00:59:16.500
Some of you might think
this is a little bit silly

00:59:16.500 --> 00:59:18.540
as a tool for focus and concentration,

00:59:18.540 --> 00:59:21.600
but if you look at the data on epinephrine

00:59:21.600 --> 00:59:24.120
and how powerfully it can increase focus,

00:59:24.120 --> 00:59:26.040
I think you'd be very impressed.

00:59:26.040 --> 00:59:28.500
I mean it certainly can
increase one's ability

00:59:28.500 --> 00:59:30.300
to attend to specific visual stimuli,

00:59:30.300 --> 00:59:32.700
so for reading or math work, et cetera,

00:59:32.700 --> 00:59:34.140
it's going to be very useful.

00:59:34.140 --> 00:59:35.490
And of course you don't
want to make it so cold

00:59:35.490 --> 00:59:37.800
that you're shivering and
chattering the whole time.

00:59:37.800 --> 00:59:39.510
And of course you could, if you like,

00:59:39.510 --> 00:59:41.310
combine this with 40 HZ binaural beats,

00:59:41.310 --> 00:59:42.143
there's no reason

00:59:42.143 --> 00:59:44.070
why you couldn't combine
the two protocols.

00:59:44.070 --> 00:59:46.860
But the point here is that a
lot of people would love to

00:59:46.860 --> 00:59:48.690
and I think ought to leverage

00:59:48.690 --> 00:59:51.330
the health promoting and powerful effects

00:59:51.330 --> 00:59:54.540
of increasing epinephrine
on focusing concentration

00:59:54.540 --> 00:59:57.000
and running out and getting
stressed by a life event

00:59:57.000 --> 00:59:59.190
or getting into an argument
or something like that

00:59:59.190 --> 01:00:01.470
simply as a way to increase
focus and concentration

01:00:01.470 --> 01:00:03.060
doesn't seem that adaptive to me.

01:00:03.060 --> 01:00:04.290
So deliberate cold exposure

01:00:04.290 --> 01:00:05.820
is a straightforward way to do that,

01:00:05.820 --> 01:00:07.290
it doesn't involve anyone else,

01:00:07.290 --> 01:00:08.610
I suppose you could do
it with somebody else,

01:00:08.610 --> 01:00:10.260
but it doesn't require anyone else.

01:00:10.260 --> 01:00:12.750
And again, there are zero low

01:00:12.750 --> 01:00:14.970
and even negative cost
ways to approach that.

01:00:14.970 --> 01:00:16.260
If you'd like to know how long

01:00:16.260 --> 01:00:19.350
the positive effects of epinephrine last

01:00:19.350 --> 01:00:21.570
toward improving focus and concentration,

01:00:21.570 --> 01:00:23.520
well, if we look to that
study from DeGroote et al,

01:00:23.520 --> 01:00:25.692
the acute stress improves
cognitive performance study,

01:00:25.692 --> 01:00:28.770
they measured concentration before

01:00:28.770 --> 01:00:31.895
and 30 minutes after
the stress was induced

01:00:31.895 --> 01:00:34.770
and there does appear to
be a quite long lasting,

01:00:34.770 --> 01:00:37.260
really up to an hour or more effect

01:00:37.260 --> 01:00:39.420
of increasing epinephrine.

01:00:39.420 --> 01:00:42.180
So how might you apply
these sorts of protocols

01:00:42.180 --> 01:00:44.550
early in the day or later in the day?

01:00:44.550 --> 01:00:47.790
Well, one suggestion or one
potential protocol would be

01:00:47.790 --> 01:00:49.590
if you're going to sit
down and do some work,

01:00:49.590 --> 01:00:51.390
if you're already feeling
alert and focused,

01:00:51.390 --> 01:00:52.710
no need to reach to this tool,

01:00:52.710 --> 01:00:55.110
but if you're feeling like
your focus and alertness

01:00:55.110 --> 01:00:56.730
isn't quite where you'd like it to be,

01:00:56.730 --> 01:00:59.010
you could take a three
minute very cold shower

01:00:59.010 --> 01:01:01.050
or submerge yourself in cold
water for three minutes,

01:01:01.050 --> 01:01:02.760
you might have a cup of coffee as well

01:01:02.760 --> 01:01:04.050
and then sit down and do that work,

01:01:04.050 --> 01:01:06.660
maybe even throw in the
40 HZ binaural beats.

01:01:06.660 --> 01:01:09.780
All of that would be layering
in the different systems,

01:01:09.780 --> 01:01:11.340
the different neurochemicals,

01:01:11.340 --> 01:01:15.060
such as acetylcholine,
epinephrine and dopamine,

01:01:15.060 --> 01:01:16.080
that are going to lend themselves

01:01:16.080 --> 01:01:19.320
to a really terrific 90
minute or less work about.

01:01:19.320 --> 01:01:22.170
Now I'd like to discuss some
of the purely behavioral tools

01:01:22.170 --> 01:01:24.210
that quality peer reviewed science say

01:01:24.210 --> 01:01:27.870
can improve focus and
concentration significantly.

01:01:27.870 --> 01:01:29.730
At the beginning of today's episode,

01:01:29.730 --> 01:01:32.430
I talked about the study
from Dr. Wendy Suzuki's lab

01:01:32.430 --> 01:01:35.220
where they explored a 13 minute meditation

01:01:35.220 --> 01:01:38.160
done every day for a
period of eight weeks.

01:01:38.160 --> 01:01:40.650
That meditation led to
significant improvements

01:01:40.650 --> 01:01:42.840
in focus and concentration ability,

01:01:42.840 --> 01:01:45.480
as well as other aspects
of cognitive performance.

01:01:45.480 --> 01:01:48.540
It also improved mood and reduced stress.

01:01:48.540 --> 01:01:51.300
So you might be wondering what
exactly is this meditation?

01:01:51.300 --> 01:01:52.950
The meditation is very simple

01:01:52.950 --> 01:01:55.200
and it's one that anyone can perform.

01:01:55.200 --> 01:01:57.390
What you would want to do is set a timer

01:01:57.390 --> 01:01:58.860
for about 13 minutes,

01:01:58.860 --> 01:02:00.810
I don't think it has to
be exactly 13 minutes,

01:02:00.810 --> 01:02:02.986
but since that's what they
included in the study,

01:02:02.986 --> 01:02:05.730
you would set a timer for 13 minutes.

01:02:05.730 --> 01:02:09.120
You would sit or lie
down, close your eyes,

01:02:09.120 --> 01:02:12.240
and you would simply
focus on your breathing.

01:02:12.240 --> 01:02:13.500
Most people are going to benefit

01:02:13.500 --> 01:02:15.900
from only doing that
breathing through their nose,

01:02:15.900 --> 01:02:18.090
but if you have some sort of obstruction

01:02:18.090 --> 01:02:19.710
or inability to breathe
just through your nose,

01:02:19.710 --> 01:02:20.760
you could probably also do it

01:02:20.760 --> 01:02:23.430
by breathing through your nose
and mouth or just your mouth.

01:02:23.430 --> 01:02:25.890
But ideally you would
do just nasal breathing

01:02:25.890 --> 01:02:27.180
for a period of 13 minutes,

01:02:27.180 --> 01:02:28.860
concentrating on that breathing,

01:02:28.860 --> 01:02:32.220
and concentrating, meaning
bringing your awareness,

01:02:32.220 --> 01:02:34.410
your so-called interoceptive awareness

01:02:34.410 --> 01:02:37.050
if you wanted to get
really technical about it,

01:02:37.050 --> 01:02:38.760
your interoceptive awareness

01:02:38.760 --> 01:02:42.240
to a point just about an
inch inside of your forehead.

01:02:42.240 --> 01:02:43.073
Now, of course,

01:02:43.073 --> 01:02:44.954
that might sound kind
of gory to some of you,

01:02:44.954 --> 01:02:47.880
you've never actually
been inside your forehead,

01:02:47.880 --> 01:02:50.220
but just about an inch
behind your forehead

01:02:50.220 --> 01:02:52.350
is where you would want to
place your concentration

01:02:52.350 --> 01:02:54.810
while also concentrating
on your breathing.

01:02:54.810 --> 01:02:56.970
Now here's the thing about meditation

01:02:56.970 --> 01:02:59.820
that all studies of meditation show,

01:02:59.820 --> 01:03:04.080
which is that unless you are
a very experienced meditator,

01:03:04.080 --> 01:03:06.030
your concentration, your focus

01:03:06.030 --> 01:03:08.760
will drift away from your breathing

01:03:08.760 --> 01:03:13.200
and away from that location
about an inch inside your head,

01:03:13.200 --> 01:03:17.011
inside your brain, about
just behind your forehead.

01:03:17.011 --> 01:03:20.940
That will happen maybe every
10 seconds, every 20 seconds,

01:03:20.940 --> 01:03:22.650
maybe even every five seconds,

01:03:22.650 --> 01:03:25.680
but an important part of
such a meditation practice

01:03:25.680 --> 01:03:27.630
to improve concentration and focus

01:03:27.630 --> 01:03:30.775
is that you are continually refocusing

01:03:30.775 --> 01:03:32.700
back to that specific location

01:03:32.700 --> 01:03:35.115
and refocusing back on your breath.

01:03:35.115 --> 01:03:37.920
This is something that again,
is not often discussed.

01:03:37.920 --> 01:03:39.540
People think that if you do a meditation

01:03:39.540 --> 01:03:41.400
and you're supposed to
concentrate on your breath,

01:03:41.400 --> 01:03:42.450
that if your mind drifts

01:03:42.450 --> 01:03:44.340
that somehow you failed
in that meditation,

01:03:44.340 --> 01:03:46.170
but actually that's not the case.

01:03:46.170 --> 01:03:49.650
A huge component of improving your ability

01:03:49.650 --> 01:03:52.740
to focus and concentrate
by way of neuroplasticity,

01:03:52.740 --> 01:03:55.860
rewiring of the circuits
for focus and concentration,

01:03:55.860 --> 01:03:59.520
is the repeated return to a state of focus

01:03:59.520 --> 01:04:03.210
from a state of non-focus
or diminished focus.

01:04:03.210 --> 01:04:06.239
So think about it like trying
to drive down the freeway

01:04:06.239 --> 01:04:09.510
and staying between the
lane lines, excuse me.

01:04:09.510 --> 01:04:10.590
And every once in a while,

01:04:10.590 --> 01:04:13.050
because there's a bit
of drift on the vehicle,

01:04:13.050 --> 01:04:14.490
maybe the wheels aren't aligned correctly

01:04:14.490 --> 01:04:16.320
or there's something else
wrong with the chassis

01:04:16.320 --> 01:04:17.340
or the steering device,

01:04:17.340 --> 01:04:19.080
it starts to drift right a little bit,

01:04:19.080 --> 01:04:20.670
then you hit the rumble strip to go rrrr,

01:04:20.670 --> 01:04:22.380
and then you pull back to the center.

01:04:22.380 --> 01:04:25.890
That's really what a focused
meditation practice is about

01:04:25.890 --> 01:04:28.350
as opposed to expecting yourself

01:04:28.350 --> 01:04:31.350
to stay between the mental
lane lines, so to speak.

01:04:31.350 --> 01:04:33.450
So if you're somebody who's
going to do a practice

01:04:33.450 --> 01:04:34.770
of the sort that I described,

01:04:34.770 --> 01:04:37.350
13 minute meditation practice every day,

01:04:37.350 --> 01:04:39.360
you'd want to sit or lie
down, close your eyes,

01:04:39.360 --> 01:04:40.740
start to concentrate on your breath,

01:04:40.740 --> 01:04:42.300
focus your attention

01:04:42.300 --> 01:04:45.270
on a location about an
inch behind your forehead,

01:04:45.270 --> 01:04:48.120
and then fully expect that at some point,

01:04:48.120 --> 01:04:49.830
you'll be thinking about something else,

01:04:49.830 --> 01:04:52.830
and that's a cue to focus
back to that location

01:04:52.830 --> 01:04:54.270
just about an inch behind your forehead

01:04:54.270 --> 01:04:56.220
and back to your breath.

01:04:56.220 --> 01:04:57.780
By doing that repeatedly over and over,

01:04:57.780 --> 01:04:59.400
what you're really training up

01:04:59.400 --> 01:05:02.160
is the network within your brain

01:05:02.160 --> 01:05:04.560
that indeed includes
that prefrontal cortex

01:05:04.560 --> 01:05:06.600
that you're focusing on as
well as some other structures,

01:05:06.600 --> 01:05:09.240
the inferior temporal cortex,
indeed the hippocampus,

01:05:09.240 --> 01:05:10.890
a structure associated with memory,

01:05:10.890 --> 01:05:13.230
and other components of the neural circuit

01:05:13.230 --> 01:05:14.880
that are involved in directing

01:05:14.880 --> 01:05:17.100
our mental focus and concentration.

01:05:17.100 --> 01:05:19.440
Again, I can't emphasize the importance

01:05:19.440 --> 01:05:22.890
of this practice being one
of focusing and refocusing.

01:05:22.890 --> 01:05:25.170
In fact, I would prefer
to call such a practice,

01:05:25.170 --> 01:05:29.610
a refocus focused meditation,
or a constantly refocusing,

01:05:29.610 --> 01:05:31.950
or maybe you all can come up
with a better name for it,

01:05:31.950 --> 01:05:33.870
I'm certainly not that
good at naming things,

01:05:33.870 --> 01:05:36.030
but this sort of meditation practice

01:05:36.030 --> 01:05:36.863
has been shown

01:05:36.863 --> 01:05:39.727
in the study by the Suzuki
lab and other studies

01:05:39.727 --> 01:05:42.720
to really improve people's ability

01:05:42.720 --> 01:05:45.300
to focus and remain focused,

01:05:45.300 --> 01:05:49.140
so much so that in the beautiful
book, "Altered States,"

01:05:49.140 --> 01:05:51.450
they describe a number of
different meditation practices,

01:05:51.450 --> 01:05:54.750
some a little bit longer than
the one that I described,

01:05:54.750 --> 01:05:57.030
one that's 17 minutes,
another one that's 30 minutes,

01:05:57.030 --> 01:05:59.010
some people will meditate
as long as 60 minutes a day,

01:05:59.010 --> 01:06:01.080
although that's quite a
long time, in my opinion.

01:06:01.080 --> 01:06:03.840
The point here isn't how long you focus

01:06:03.840 --> 01:06:06.870
or somehow trying to achieve total focus

01:06:06.870 --> 01:06:09.720
for the entire 13 minute or 17 minute

01:06:09.720 --> 01:06:12.390
or 60 minute about of meditation.

01:06:12.390 --> 01:06:13.740
While that would be wonderful

01:06:13.740 --> 01:06:15.810
and I think many people aspire to do that,

01:06:15.810 --> 01:06:17.310
that's a lot of hard mental work.

01:06:17.310 --> 01:06:19.710
I think for most people out
there, including myself,

01:06:19.710 --> 01:06:24.180
a relatively short meditation
practice of about 13 minutes

01:06:24.180 --> 01:06:26.401
in which you fully expect

01:06:26.401 --> 01:06:28.740
your focus and concentration to drift,

01:06:28.740 --> 01:06:30.630
but that you are continually refocusing

01:06:30.630 --> 01:06:32.520
is going to be the most effective,

01:06:32.520 --> 01:06:34.320
yes, indeed, the most effective

01:06:34.320 --> 01:06:37.740
at teaching yourself to
focus and stay concentrated.

01:06:37.740 --> 01:06:39.630
In fact, I invite you to interpret

01:06:39.630 --> 01:06:42.930
every time that you
focus off that location

01:06:42.930 --> 01:06:44.850
about one inch behind your forehead,

01:06:44.850 --> 01:06:46.350
as an opportunity to refocus

01:06:46.350 --> 01:06:49.290
and think about the
refocusing as the trigger

01:06:49.290 --> 01:06:51.000
for teaching your neural circuits

01:06:51.000 --> 01:06:53.610
how to focus for extended periods of time.

01:06:53.610 --> 01:06:55.773
And as a bonus to that sort
of meditation practice,

01:06:55.773 --> 01:06:57.930
the study from Wendy Suzuki's lab

01:06:57.930 --> 01:07:01.140
also showed that people
experience improvements in sleep

01:07:01.140 --> 01:07:02.580
and improvements in memory,

01:07:02.580 --> 01:07:05.040
so not just improvements in
mood and reduction in stress

01:07:05.040 --> 01:07:07.440
and improvements in
focus and concentration,

01:07:07.440 --> 01:07:09.120
but all these other positive benefits

01:07:09.120 --> 01:07:12.240
from just doing that 13 minute
a day meditation practice.

01:07:12.240 --> 01:07:14.070
It's one that I've started to adopt

01:07:14.070 --> 01:07:15.477
and have felt tremendous benefit from

01:07:15.477 --> 01:07:18.180
and that I encourage many
of you to try as well.

01:07:18.180 --> 01:07:19.260
The one cautionary note

01:07:19.260 --> 01:07:21.180
is the one that I mentioned at
the beginning of the episode,

01:07:21.180 --> 01:07:25.950
which is because the refocus
as I'll call it, meditation,

01:07:25.950 --> 01:07:28.500
does involve a significant
amount of effort

01:07:28.500 --> 01:07:31.080
and engagement of these
prefrontal cortical circuits,

01:07:31.080 --> 01:07:35.040
it is disruptive to sleep if
performed too closely to sleep.

01:07:35.040 --> 01:07:36.660
So if you are going to do that practice,

01:07:36.660 --> 01:07:38.400
I recommend that you not do it

01:07:38.400 --> 01:07:40.800
within the four hours
prior to your bedtime.

01:07:40.800 --> 01:07:41.633
Earlier I mentioned

01:07:41.633 --> 01:07:43.682
that I would talk about
ways to improve focus

01:07:43.682 --> 01:07:45.450
if you are sleep deprived.

01:07:45.450 --> 01:07:47.760
This is something that
I'm all too familiar with.

01:07:47.760 --> 01:07:50.250
I put a lot of effort
into optimizing my sleep,

01:07:50.250 --> 01:07:51.840
that's something that
with each passing year,

01:07:51.840 --> 01:07:53.370
I put more and more effort into,

01:07:53.370 --> 01:07:54.930
again, because sleep is so vital

01:07:54.930 --> 01:07:56.310
for mental health, physical health

01:07:56.310 --> 01:07:58.080
and performance of all kinds,

01:07:58.080 --> 01:08:00.840
but certainly in my role as a student,

01:08:00.840 --> 01:08:03.930
in my role as a professor
and in my role in life,

01:08:03.930 --> 01:08:05.850
I've had numerous times

01:08:05.850 --> 01:08:07.920
in which I simply did not get enough sleep

01:08:07.920 --> 01:08:10.980
or my sleep was terrible
for whatever reason,

01:08:10.980 --> 01:08:13.530
and yet I still had work
demands and social demands,

01:08:13.530 --> 01:08:14.363
et cetera.

01:08:15.300 --> 01:08:17.070
One practice that is very effective

01:08:17.070 --> 01:08:18.780
at allowing you to focus

01:08:18.780 --> 01:08:20.970
better than you would otherwise

01:08:20.970 --> 01:08:23.010
under conditions of sleep deprivation

01:08:23.010 --> 01:08:26.280
is so called non-sleep deep rest or NSDR.

01:08:26.280 --> 01:08:29.100
This is also referred to
sometimes as yoga nidra.

01:08:29.100 --> 01:08:31.110
Yoga nidra actually means yoga sleep.

01:08:31.110 --> 01:08:35.190
Yoga nidra is a practice of
lying down for about 10 to 30

01:08:35.190 --> 01:08:37.260
sometimes even as long as 60 minutes,

01:08:37.260 --> 01:08:39.720
you listen to a script,
it's an audio script,

01:08:39.720 --> 01:08:42.390
that takes you through a
progressive deep relaxation,

01:08:42.390 --> 01:08:43.770
it involves a body scan,

01:08:43.770 --> 01:08:45.240
some long exhale breathing.

01:08:45.240 --> 01:08:48.600
It is very restorative in the
sense that one tends to emerge

01:08:48.600 --> 01:08:51.620
from yoga nidra or NSDR
feeling greatly refreshed

01:08:51.620 --> 01:08:54.900
compared to how you felt prior to it.

01:08:54.900 --> 01:08:57.270
There is also terrific neuroimaging data

01:08:57.270 --> 01:08:59.850
from laboratories in Denmark

01:08:59.850 --> 01:09:02.280
showing that there's a
restoration of dopamine levels

01:09:02.280 --> 01:09:06.480
in the so-called basal ganglia
after NSDR AKA yoga nidra.

01:09:06.480 --> 01:09:09.210
Whether or not you call
it yoga nidra or NSDR,

01:09:09.210 --> 01:09:11.850
which is what I refer to
it as, non-sleep deep rest,

01:09:11.850 --> 01:09:15.390
you can find these scripts at
zero cost in multiple places.

01:09:15.390 --> 01:09:17.990
You can find there are
certain apps that are NSDR

01:09:17.990 --> 01:09:20.040
or yoga nidra apps.

01:09:20.040 --> 01:09:22.106
There is a NSDR protocol

01:09:22.106 --> 01:09:25.560
that was put out there by
Madefor which is on YouTube

01:09:25.560 --> 01:09:27.150
that you can access for free.

01:09:27.150 --> 01:09:31.050
There is a NSDR or I should
say a number of NSDR protocols

01:09:31.050 --> 01:09:32.850
through the Virtusan app.

01:09:32.850 --> 01:09:34.710
There are, again, number
of different places

01:09:34.710 --> 01:09:37.710
that one can access NSDR protocols.

01:09:37.710 --> 01:09:42.240
I do NSDR for 10 to 30 minutes
per day every single day,

01:09:42.240 --> 01:09:44.520
not just on days where I'm sleep deprived.

01:09:44.520 --> 01:09:45.840
If I happen to be sleep deprived,

01:09:45.840 --> 01:09:49.290
I would extend that NSDR
to 30 or 60 minutes.

01:09:49.290 --> 01:09:50.760
And when you do that NSDR

01:09:50.760 --> 01:09:53.400
will depend on when you
have time for that NSDR.

01:09:53.400 --> 01:09:54.330
When I haven't slept well,

01:09:54.330 --> 01:09:56.340
what I'll try and do is find a quiet place

01:09:56.340 --> 01:09:59.927
where I can do NSDR for
30 or ideally 60 minutes.

01:09:59.927 --> 01:10:02.580
Sometimes I will fall back
asleep during that NSDR,

01:10:02.580 --> 01:10:04.740
that's fine if you do that,

01:10:04.740 --> 01:10:07.484
but most people will stay
awake during the NSDR,

01:10:07.484 --> 01:10:10.260
and then I'll emerge from
that and go about my day.

01:10:10.260 --> 01:10:11.490
If in the afternoon,

01:10:11.490 --> 01:10:13.320
I'm very fatigued
because of lack of sleep,

01:10:13.320 --> 01:10:17.520
I might do another NSDR
of 10 to 30 or 60 minutes,

01:10:17.520 --> 01:10:18.840
and then another work about.

01:10:18.840 --> 01:10:21.420
Again, NSDR is something I do every day.

01:10:21.420 --> 01:10:24.240
I talked a lot about this in
the episodes related to sleep,

01:10:24.240 --> 01:10:25.560
because it can help you get better

01:10:25.560 --> 01:10:28.020
at falling and staying asleep at night

01:10:28.020 --> 01:10:30.990
in addition to feeling restorative

01:10:30.990 --> 01:10:32.700
in that immediate timeframe of the day

01:10:32.700 --> 01:10:33.960
in which you do NSDR,

01:10:33.960 --> 01:10:35.970
so it's immensely
beneficial at various times

01:10:35.970 --> 01:10:37.530
and for various purposes.

01:10:37.530 --> 01:10:39.060
But here within the context

01:10:39.060 --> 01:10:40.530
of trying to concentrate and focus

01:10:40.530 --> 01:10:42.300
when you're sleep deprived,

01:10:42.300 --> 01:10:46.920
NSDR, AKA yoga nidra is an
immensely beneficial practice.

01:10:46.920 --> 01:10:49.410
There's growing amounts of quality science

01:10:49.410 --> 01:10:52.770
pointing to the neurochemical
replenishing effects,

01:10:52.770 --> 01:10:54.450
as I mentioned before, dopamine,

01:10:54.450 --> 01:10:57.330
but also the potential for NSDR

01:10:57.330 --> 01:10:59.280
to replace sleep that you've lost.

01:10:59.280 --> 01:11:01.220
I would never want anyone
to try and use NSDR

01:11:01.220 --> 01:11:03.120
as a total replacement for sleep,

01:11:03.120 --> 01:11:05.910
but under conditions in which
you cannot control your sleep,

01:11:05.910 --> 01:11:08.640
NSDR is going to be the
best way that I am aware of

01:11:08.640 --> 01:11:11.670
to restore your ability
to focus and concentrate

01:11:11.670 --> 01:11:13.080
for whatever purpose.

01:11:13.080 --> 01:11:14.850
And if you emerge from your NSDR

01:11:14.850 --> 01:11:15.930
and then drink some caffeine,

01:11:15.930 --> 01:11:18.340
you'll notice an even greater capacity

01:11:18.340 --> 01:11:20.190
for focus and concentration

01:11:20.190 --> 01:11:22.710
for all the reasons directly
related to caffeine.

01:11:22.710 --> 01:11:25.710
So again, NSDR is a general tool

01:11:25.710 --> 01:11:27.827
for enhancing your ability to sleep

01:11:27.827 --> 01:11:29.760
and it's a tool that you can use

01:11:29.760 --> 01:11:32.880
in order to compensate for,
at least to some degree,

01:11:32.880 --> 01:11:34.740
compensate for lost sleep

01:11:34.740 --> 01:11:36.660
when you need to focus and concentrate.

01:11:36.660 --> 01:11:39.840
One thing that really
contrasts NSDR and yoga nidra

01:11:39.840 --> 01:11:41.700
with the sort of focused meditation

01:11:41.700 --> 01:11:42.990
that I talked about a few minutes ago,

01:11:42.990 --> 01:11:44.760
the 13 minute meditation,

01:11:44.760 --> 01:11:46.650
is that during the 13 minute meditation,

01:11:46.650 --> 01:11:48.930
you're actively trying
to refocus and focus,

01:11:48.930 --> 01:11:50.670
whereas during NSDR and yoga nidra,

01:11:50.670 --> 01:11:52.770
you're actually trying to defocus.

01:11:52.770 --> 01:11:54.810
So you can think of the
13 minute meditation

01:11:54.810 --> 01:11:56.730
for refocusing and focusing

01:11:56.730 --> 01:12:00.450
as directly tapping into and mediating

01:12:00.450 --> 01:12:02.940
improvements in the circuitry
for focus and concentration

01:12:02.940 --> 01:12:05.970
whereas you can think
of NSDR and yoga nidra

01:12:05.970 --> 01:12:08.790
as modulating your brain and body state

01:12:08.790 --> 01:12:12.210
to allow you to focus
and concentrate better.

01:12:12.210 --> 01:12:14.040
Now another tool that you can use

01:12:14.040 --> 01:12:15.110
to directly tap in

01:12:15.110 --> 01:12:17.250
to the circuits for
focus and concentration

01:12:17.250 --> 01:12:22.020
and to greatly accelerate
neuroplasticity, the improvements,

01:12:22.020 --> 01:12:23.790
or I should say the
changes in those circuits

01:12:23.790 --> 01:12:26.160
that will allow you to
focus and concentrate better

01:12:26.160 --> 01:12:27.240
is hypnosis.

01:12:27.240 --> 01:12:29.640
A lot of people hear hypnosis
and they think stage hypnosis,

01:12:29.640 --> 01:12:31.020
you know, people squawking like chickens

01:12:31.020 --> 01:12:32.610
and doing things against their will,

01:12:32.610 --> 01:12:35.940
but actually hypnosis is a atypical

01:12:35.940 --> 01:12:37.470
but highly accessible brain state

01:12:37.470 --> 01:12:40.072
that's been studied with a lot of rigor

01:12:40.072 --> 01:12:42.630
at Stanford University School of Medicine

01:12:42.630 --> 01:12:44.340
by my colleague, Dr. David Spiegel,

01:12:44.340 --> 01:12:46.530
he's been a guest on
this podcast previously.

01:12:46.530 --> 01:12:48.210
Hypnosis is a unique brain state

01:12:48.210 --> 01:12:51.060
because it's one in which
you are deeply focused

01:12:51.060 --> 01:12:52.620
and yet deeply relaxed.

01:12:52.620 --> 01:12:55.140
So to just sort of set up
the array of practices here

01:12:55.140 --> 01:12:56.880
so you can think about them logically,

01:12:56.880 --> 01:12:58.530
the focus refocus meditation

01:12:58.530 --> 01:13:02.580
is based on and focused
on focus, no pun intended.

01:13:02.580 --> 01:13:06.780
NSDR and yoga nidra are
aimed at deep relaxation.

01:13:06.780 --> 01:13:10.380
Hypnosis is this atypical,
very powerful brain state

01:13:10.380 --> 01:13:13.260
in which you combine high levels of focus

01:13:13.260 --> 01:13:15.310
and deep relaxation.

01:13:15.310 --> 01:13:16.890
Now it's a little bit of a tough one

01:13:16.890 --> 01:13:18.510
to just take oneself into,

01:13:18.510 --> 01:13:20.010
but fortunately, there's a tool

01:13:20.010 --> 01:13:22.200
based on a lot of quality
peer-reviewed research

01:13:22.200 --> 01:13:23.670
from the Spiegel lab and other labs

01:13:23.670 --> 01:13:26.640
and that is the Reveri app, r-e-v-e-r-i.

01:13:26.640 --> 01:13:30.240
The Reveri app is available for no cost,

01:13:30.240 --> 01:13:31.073
at least for a period of time

01:13:31.073 --> 01:13:33.450
and then I think they place
certain elements of it

01:13:33.450 --> 01:13:34.620
behind a pay wall,

01:13:34.620 --> 01:13:37.080
but you can try at zero cost,

01:13:37.080 --> 01:13:38.130
it's available for Apple,

01:13:38.130 --> 01:13:40.650
soon I think also to be
available for Android,

01:13:40.650 --> 01:13:43.320
and they have specific hypnosis protocols

01:13:43.320 --> 01:13:45.997
that you listen to, and these
are very brief protocols,

01:13:45.997 --> 01:13:47.970
follow the instructions,

01:13:47.970 --> 01:13:49.740
you're listening to a
particular audio script

01:13:49.740 --> 01:13:51.450
of David Spiegel himself,

01:13:51.450 --> 01:13:53.010
and some progressive breathing

01:13:53.010 --> 01:13:54.600
and actually some eye movements

01:13:54.600 --> 01:13:56.820
that are directly linked
to the neural circuits

01:13:56.820 --> 01:13:59.760
that allow for these highly
focused, deeply relaxed states.

01:13:59.760 --> 01:14:02.160
And there are components
within the Reveri app

01:14:02.160 --> 01:14:03.090
specifically geared

01:14:03.090 --> 01:14:06.000
towards improving focus and concentration.

01:14:06.000 --> 01:14:07.200
So again,

01:14:07.200 --> 01:14:08.940
there's meditation for focus,

01:14:08.940 --> 01:14:11.970
there's deliberate
decompression, NSDR, yoga nidra,

01:14:11.970 --> 01:14:13.530
which take you into deep relaxation,

01:14:13.530 --> 01:14:17.610
and then hypnosis is this very
special, very directed state

01:14:17.610 --> 01:14:20.880
of highly focused and highly relaxed,

01:14:20.880 --> 01:14:22.470
or I should say deeply relaxed,

01:14:22.470 --> 01:14:24.660
that allow access to the neural circuits

01:14:24.660 --> 01:14:26.370
for focus and concentration

01:14:26.370 --> 01:14:29.160
and allow you to tune those
up and to improve those

01:14:29.160 --> 01:14:32.040
very significantly in a
very brief amount of time.

01:14:32.040 --> 01:14:33.900
And again, some of those hypnosis scripts

01:14:33.900 --> 01:14:35.100
are as short as eight minutes,

01:14:35.100 --> 01:14:36.870
some are as long as 13 minutes.

01:14:36.870 --> 01:14:40.140
So what we're really talking
about here are zero cost tools

01:14:40.140 --> 01:14:42.540
that directly tap into
the neural circuits,

01:14:42.540 --> 01:14:44.130
the components within your brain,

01:14:44.130 --> 01:14:47.520
that allow for deep relaxation,
allow for deep focus,

01:14:47.520 --> 01:14:51.030
and improve your ability to
focus and concentrate over time

01:14:51.030 --> 01:14:53.010
simply by repeating these.

01:14:53.010 --> 01:14:56.700
How often do you need to
repeat the Reveri hypnosis

01:14:56.700 --> 01:14:58.950
for focus and concentration
before you see benefits?

01:14:58.950 --> 01:15:00.919
Well, that will vary
from person to person.

01:15:00.919 --> 01:15:03.990
I tend to use it once
every third or fourth day

01:15:03.990 --> 01:15:06.264
and have experienced
tremendous benefits from it.

01:15:06.264 --> 01:15:08.070
I don't think I'm unique in that sense,

01:15:08.070 --> 01:15:10.860
they have a lot of data
to support this Reveri app

01:15:10.860 --> 01:15:12.898
and the protocols within it.

01:15:12.898 --> 01:15:15.210
How long do you have to do NSDR

01:15:15.210 --> 01:15:16.680
before you experience those benefits?

01:15:16.680 --> 01:15:18.330
There, I would say the
first time and every time,

01:15:18.330 --> 01:15:19.860
because it's so deeply relaxing

01:15:19.860 --> 01:15:21.990
that you emerge from it
feeling quite restored

01:15:21.990 --> 01:15:23.653
relative to how you went into it.

01:15:23.653 --> 01:15:27.000
And as I mentioned earlier
in the study on meditation,

01:15:27.000 --> 01:15:27.900
it took about eight weeks

01:15:27.900 --> 01:15:30.480
to see the effects that
they observed in that study,

01:15:30.480 --> 01:15:32.460
but they didn't observe
shorter time points.

01:15:32.460 --> 01:15:36.030
So I highly encourage
people to explore meditation

01:15:36.030 --> 01:15:37.440
geared towards focus and refocus,

01:15:37.440 --> 01:15:40.590
also NSDR, nonsleep deep
rest, AKA yoga nidra,

01:15:40.590 --> 01:15:41.610
and the Reveri app,

01:15:41.610 --> 01:15:44.242
Specifically the hypnosis
within the Reveri app,

01:15:44.242 --> 01:15:47.340
that's geared towards improving
focus and concentration.

01:15:47.340 --> 01:15:49.740
All of these have terrific
science to support them,

01:15:49.740 --> 01:15:52.110
this is not woo science or hacks

01:15:52.110 --> 01:15:53.880
or just something that
people came up with.

01:15:53.880 --> 01:15:55.470
This is all grounded in work

01:15:55.470 --> 01:15:58.170
from some of the best
universities in the world,

01:15:58.170 --> 01:15:59.580
from excellent groups

01:15:59.580 --> 01:16:01.440
who've looked at underlying
neural mechanisms

01:16:01.440 --> 01:16:03.150
and measured things with a lot of rigor,

01:16:03.150 --> 01:16:04.140
et cetera, et cetera.

01:16:04.140 --> 01:16:05.400
These tools are available to you,

01:16:05.400 --> 01:16:07.530
I highly recommend that you use them.

01:16:07.530 --> 01:16:08.370
And if you're interested

01:16:08.370 --> 01:16:10.470
in the optimal time of day to do these,

01:16:10.470 --> 01:16:14.400
we already mentioned that
the focus refocus meditation

01:16:14.400 --> 01:16:16.440
shouldn't be done too close to sleep,

01:16:16.440 --> 01:16:20.580
the Reveri hypnosis app can
be done at any time really,

01:16:20.580 --> 01:16:21.960
in fact, there's a component

01:16:21.960 --> 01:16:23.670
of falling back of sleep in there,

01:16:23.670 --> 01:16:25.410
in other words, a hypnosis
specifically geared

01:16:25.410 --> 01:16:27.960
toward helping people teach
themselves to fall back asleep

01:16:27.960 --> 01:16:29.430
when they wake up in
the middle of the night.

01:16:29.430 --> 01:16:30.600
NSDR, I always say,

01:16:30.600 --> 01:16:32.640
can be done first thing in
the morning, in the afternoon

01:16:32.640 --> 01:16:33.540
or any time of day,

01:16:33.540 --> 01:16:34.373
and in fact,

01:16:34.373 --> 01:16:35.520
I'll sometimes do that in
the middle of the night

01:16:35.520 --> 01:16:38.100
if I happen to wake up and
need to get back to sleep.

01:16:38.100 --> 01:16:41.340
So really these tools can be
applied most any time of day,

01:16:41.340 --> 01:16:42.810
except for that one caveat

01:16:42.810 --> 01:16:44.580
about the focus refocus meditation

01:16:44.580 --> 01:16:46.530
not being done too close to sleep.

01:16:46.530 --> 01:16:48.480
Now there's another
set of behavioral tools

01:16:48.480 --> 01:16:51.420
that can really help enhance
one's ability to focus

01:16:51.420 --> 01:16:53.614
and those are visual based tools.

01:16:53.614 --> 01:16:55.830
In fact, the tools I'm about to describe

01:16:55.830 --> 01:16:58.770
are actually being employed
in a number of schools

01:16:58.770 --> 01:17:01.680
in China and elsewhere in
order to teach children

01:17:01.680 --> 01:17:04.743
to focus better and for
longer periods of time.

01:17:05.670 --> 01:17:10.590
The key principle here is that
much of our cognitive focus,

01:17:10.590 --> 01:17:13.110
our ability to think about
something in a very specific way

01:17:13.110 --> 01:17:14.550
and stay focused on it,

01:17:14.550 --> 01:17:18.090
to read or to follow
a line of conversation

01:17:18.090 --> 01:17:20.250
or math or music, et cetera,

01:17:20.250 --> 01:17:24.030
is going to be directed
by our visual system.

01:17:24.030 --> 01:17:26.520
Our visual system has two
forms of attention and focus.

01:17:26.520 --> 01:17:29.430
One is overt focus, which
is very straightforward.

01:17:29.430 --> 01:17:30.780
If I'm looking at the tip of my pen,

01:17:30.780 --> 01:17:31.800
for those of who are listening right now,

01:17:31.800 --> 01:17:33.840
I'm looking at the tip of
my pen, that's overt focus,

01:17:33.840 --> 01:17:35.610
I'm focusing on it with my eyes,

01:17:35.610 --> 01:17:38.220
and of course, the rest of
my brain then will follow

01:17:38.220 --> 01:17:40.377
and start to analyze the
details of what I'm seeing

01:17:40.377 --> 01:17:42.630
and the contours of the pen, et cetera.

01:17:42.630 --> 01:17:44.670
It seems sort of obvious
when you first hear it,

01:17:44.670 --> 01:17:47.220
but our cognitive focus tends to follow

01:17:47.220 --> 01:17:49.530
our overt visual focus.

01:17:49.530 --> 01:17:51.780
That's also why they
put blinders on horses.

01:17:51.780 --> 01:17:54.960
That's also why sometimes
wearing a hoodie or a hat

01:17:54.960 --> 01:17:57.301
or limiting your visual field in some way

01:17:57.301 --> 01:18:00.140
can help you enhance your cognitive focus,

01:18:00.140 --> 01:18:01.650
it can help limit distraction.

01:18:01.650 --> 01:18:03.870
You're just not seeing as much.

01:18:03.870 --> 01:18:05.700
It's also why when we ingest caffeine

01:18:05.700 --> 01:18:07.590
or any kind of stimulant
or we are stressed

01:18:07.590 --> 01:18:08.910
and our pupils dilate

01:18:08.910 --> 01:18:10.560
and our vision becomes more tunnel-like,

01:18:10.560 --> 01:18:12.420
less panoramic, but more tunnel-like,

01:18:12.420 --> 01:18:14.220
you know they say a soda
straw view of the world

01:18:14.220 --> 01:18:15.794
or you're looking through a tunnel,

01:18:15.794 --> 01:18:18.150
your focus, your visual focus

01:18:18.150 --> 01:18:19.920
is actually driving your cognitive focus.

01:18:19.920 --> 01:18:23.100
Your cognitive focus is
narrower than it would be

01:18:23.100 --> 01:18:24.960
if you were seeing the
whole scene that you're in.

01:18:24.960 --> 01:18:26.400
So when you hear this, it sounds obvious,

01:18:26.400 --> 01:18:28.620
but for many people,
including many scientists,

01:18:28.620 --> 01:18:30.420
it's just not obvious that
this would be the case.

01:18:30.420 --> 01:18:31.890
However, that is the case,

01:18:31.890 --> 01:18:34.800
your visual focus drives
your cognitive focus.

01:18:34.800 --> 01:18:36.480
So what is a practice

01:18:36.480 --> 01:18:39.570
that has been studied
in various laboratories

01:18:39.570 --> 01:18:41.190
and that's being employed
in various schools?

01:18:41.190 --> 01:18:46.190
Is to have children or adults
visually focus on one location

01:18:46.980 --> 01:18:48.570
for a given period of time.

01:18:48.570 --> 01:18:49.560
How long?

01:18:49.560 --> 01:18:52.290
Anywhere from 30 seconds to three minutes,

01:18:52.290 --> 01:18:53.123
and believe it or not,

01:18:53.123 --> 01:18:53.956
three minutes is a long time

01:18:53.956 --> 01:18:56.666
to maintain visual focus at one location.

01:18:56.666 --> 01:18:58.140
If you were to try that right now,

01:18:58.140 --> 01:19:00.150
you'd probably find it
to be a bit of a strain,

01:19:00.150 --> 01:19:01.800
but if you want to try it, you can.

01:19:01.800 --> 01:19:05.100
Keep in mind, you yes,
are allowed to blink,

01:19:05.100 --> 01:19:08.700
but also keep in mind that
meditation refocusing practice

01:19:08.700 --> 01:19:10.290
that we talked about earlier,

01:19:10.290 --> 01:19:13.560
that the refocusing is the key component

01:19:13.560 --> 01:19:15.841
of teaching yourself or your brain,

01:19:15.841 --> 01:19:17.430
you are your brain, your brain is you,

01:19:17.430 --> 01:19:19.860
but to teach yourself how to focus better.

01:19:19.860 --> 01:19:23.190
So if you're going to
incorporate this practice,

01:19:23.190 --> 01:19:25.350
what you would want to
do is pick a location,

01:19:25.350 --> 01:19:26.460
it could be on a wall,

01:19:26.460 --> 01:19:28.230
it could be on your
computer in front of you,

01:19:28.230 --> 01:19:29.130
although I would encourage it

01:19:29.130 --> 01:19:31.020
to not be the contents
of your computer screen,

01:19:31.020 --> 01:19:32.670
you might just want to blank your screen,

01:19:32.670 --> 01:19:33.870
you might want to put a piece of paper

01:19:33.870 --> 01:19:36.660
with a crosshatch there,
any sort of visual target,

01:19:36.660 --> 01:19:38.100
or you can imagine a visual target

01:19:38.100 --> 01:19:40.500
and then focus your visual
attention on that target

01:19:40.500 --> 01:19:43.020
and try to breathe normally,
try and stay relaxed,

01:19:43.020 --> 01:19:44.430
and certainly allow yourself to blink

01:19:44.430 --> 01:19:45.870
so that your eyes don't try out.

01:19:45.870 --> 01:19:49.230
This is not a test of how long
you can go without blinking.

01:19:49.230 --> 01:19:51.900
By focusing on that particular location

01:19:51.900 --> 01:19:54.450
and by forcing yourself to
refocus on that location

01:19:54.450 --> 01:19:58.770
anytime your gaze, your vision
drifts from that location,

01:19:58.770 --> 01:20:02.130
you are encouraging the circuits for focus

01:20:02.130 --> 01:20:04.350
to get better at focusing
for longer and longer

01:20:04.350 --> 01:20:06.210
and at refocusing when your focus

01:20:06.210 --> 01:20:08.040
drifts off of that location.

01:20:08.040 --> 01:20:09.600
This is incorporating neural circuits,

01:20:09.600 --> 01:20:11.040
including the prefrontal cortex,

01:20:11.040 --> 01:20:12.570
things like the frontal eye fields.

01:20:12.570 --> 01:20:14.748
For those of you curious
about the underlying biology,

01:20:14.748 --> 01:20:18.000
this practice is
recruiting certain elements

01:20:18.000 --> 01:20:19.500
of your so-called prefrontal cortex,

01:20:19.500 --> 01:20:21.480
also the frontal eye fields,

01:20:21.480 --> 01:20:24.150
which are locations not far
from the prefrontal cortex,

01:20:24.150 --> 01:20:26.640
that are involved in
deliberately directing your gaze

01:20:26.640 --> 01:20:29.190
to particular locations in space,

01:20:29.190 --> 01:20:30.090
not outer space,

01:20:30.090 --> 01:20:32.820
although you could do this by
focusing on stars I suppose,

01:20:32.820 --> 01:20:35.100
but in visual space.

01:20:35.100 --> 01:20:36.480
Now I mentioned before

01:20:36.480 --> 01:20:39.870
that this is overt visual
focus and attention,

01:20:39.870 --> 01:20:42.030
you are overtly looking at that location,

01:20:42.030 --> 01:20:44.400
but one also very powerful practice

01:20:44.400 --> 01:20:48.060
for improving focus and concentration

01:20:48.060 --> 01:20:50.610
is to use covert focus.

01:20:50.610 --> 01:20:53.190
Covert focus is where my gaze,

01:20:53.190 --> 01:20:56.310
my eyes are focused on one
location, such as my pen,

01:20:56.310 --> 01:20:58.770
but my focus is actually directed

01:20:58.770 --> 01:21:01.500
elsewhere in the room
or location that I'm in.

01:21:01.500 --> 01:21:05.580
My mind and to some extent
my peripheral vision

01:21:05.580 --> 01:21:08.130
is focused in this case on
the door just to my left

01:21:08.130 --> 01:21:09.360
in the room that I'm in.

01:21:09.360 --> 01:21:10.740
That takes a little bit more effort.

01:21:10.740 --> 01:21:12.750
This is something that
all old world primates,

01:21:12.750 --> 01:21:15.630
of which we are old world
primates, are able to do.

01:21:15.630 --> 01:21:18.030
And it probably evolved
as part of the mechanism

01:21:18.030 --> 01:21:21.870
by which animals could
evaluate their scene,

01:21:21.870 --> 01:21:25.800
evaluate predators,
evaluate other primates

01:21:25.800 --> 01:21:28.620
while not necessarily
staring at them directly

01:21:28.620 --> 01:21:30.330
so they can obtain information,

01:21:30.330 --> 01:21:32.880
we can obtain information
without having to direct our gaze

01:21:32.880 --> 01:21:34.230
specifically to one location.

01:21:34.230 --> 01:21:37.140
Maybe we can obtain information
from multiple locations,

01:21:37.140 --> 01:21:38.790
indeed, we can.

01:21:38.790 --> 01:21:41.460
Without getting too far
down the rabbit hole

01:21:41.460 --> 01:21:44.640
of how vision and cognition relate,

01:21:44.640 --> 01:21:46.897
because we've done episodes
on that previously,

01:21:46.897 --> 01:21:51.720
and simply focusing on the
tools that can be incorporated

01:21:51.720 --> 01:21:55.140
to improve focus and concentration,
here's what I recommend.

01:21:55.140 --> 01:22:00.060
Set yourself a low bar
at first and set a timer

01:22:00.060 --> 01:22:03.570
and try to focus on one
location for 30 seconds

01:22:03.570 --> 01:22:05.130
and that's it for that day.

01:22:05.130 --> 01:22:07.170
The next day, you might add five seconds,

01:22:07.170 --> 01:22:09.300
and then the next day,
five seconds after that.

01:22:09.300 --> 01:22:11.190
If you miss a day, no big deal,

01:22:11.190 --> 01:22:14.520
simply do the practice for
the same amount of time

01:22:14.520 --> 01:22:16.680
that you did the last time
that you did the practice,

01:22:16.680 --> 01:22:19.800
and then gradually try and
increase the amount of time

01:22:19.800 --> 01:22:23.100
that you can focus on one
visual location overtly

01:22:23.100 --> 01:22:25.261
by looking directly at that location.

01:22:25.261 --> 01:22:28.020
If you like, and if you
feel you have the ability,

01:22:28.020 --> 01:22:30.870
you can try and do this through
covert attention and focus

01:22:30.870 --> 01:22:32.760
by looking straight forward for instance,

01:22:32.760 --> 01:22:34.560
and attending to something
in the corner of the room

01:22:34.560 --> 01:22:36.780
and trying to do that for 30 seconds,

01:22:36.780 --> 01:22:38.460
you'll find that that's
quite a bit harder,

01:22:38.460 --> 01:22:40.560
and then extending that by five seconds

01:22:40.560 --> 01:22:42.360
every time you do the practice.

01:22:42.360 --> 01:22:44.280
This is something that I
don't think you necessarily

01:22:44.280 --> 01:22:46.350
have to build up to being
able to do for a full hour

01:22:46.350 --> 01:22:47.760
in order to extract the benefits.

01:22:47.760 --> 01:22:50.520
In fact, the best way to
think about this practice

01:22:50.520 --> 01:22:53.400
is as a means to get into a focused state.

01:22:53.400 --> 01:22:56.640
If you remember back
about an hour or so ago,

01:22:56.640 --> 01:22:59.040
I was talking about how focused states

01:22:59.040 --> 01:23:03.510
are not a drop all the way in
and then exit type phenomenon.

01:23:03.510 --> 01:23:05.190
We don't just drop into a focused state

01:23:05.190 --> 01:23:07.170
the same way we don't drop
into the peak performance

01:23:07.170 --> 01:23:08.850
of a workout, we warm up.

01:23:08.850 --> 01:23:12.450
So what I recommend is having a 30 second

01:23:12.450 --> 01:23:17.450
to three minute period at the
beginning of a about to focus

01:23:17.580 --> 01:23:19.590
where you're going to
do work or physical work

01:23:19.590 --> 01:23:23.490
and anchoring your vision to one location

01:23:23.490 --> 01:23:24.810
somewhere in the room

01:23:24.810 --> 01:23:26.850
or if you want to do it
covertly, you can do that,

01:23:26.850 --> 01:23:28.500
setting a timer and trying to do that

01:23:28.500 --> 01:23:30.720
for anywhere from 30
seconds to three minutes.

01:23:30.720 --> 01:23:34.980
What you're doing when
you exercise that practice

01:23:34.980 --> 01:23:38.160
is you are ramping up neural activity

01:23:38.160 --> 01:23:39.690
within the neural circuits

01:23:39.690 --> 01:23:41.910
that create focus and concentration.

01:23:41.910 --> 01:23:44.520
Then, I would stop
looking at that location

01:23:44.520 --> 01:23:45.720
or that covert location

01:23:45.720 --> 01:23:47.520
and then I would move to the
work that you're trying to do,

01:23:47.520 --> 01:23:49.710
either mental work or physical work.

01:23:49.710 --> 01:23:52.470
And if about halfway
through your 90 minute about

01:23:52.470 --> 01:23:55.200
or at some point in your 90
minute about of work or exercise,

01:23:55.200 --> 01:23:57.090
you feel that your
concentration is drifting

01:23:57.090 --> 01:23:58.380
rather than look at your phone

01:23:58.380 --> 01:24:01.080
and scroll through the
thousands of contexts

01:24:01.080 --> 01:24:03.390
that exists within social
media or your phone,

01:24:03.390 --> 01:24:05.790
try just picking a
location again on the wall,

01:24:05.790 --> 01:24:07.680
focusing back on that location,

01:24:07.680 --> 01:24:09.780
using that as a ramp up

01:24:09.780 --> 01:24:11.910
to then direct your focus back towards,

01:24:11.910 --> 01:24:12.743
if your weight training,

01:24:12.743 --> 01:24:14.130
sets and reps that you
might be performing,

01:24:14.130 --> 01:24:15.000
if you're running,

01:24:15.000 --> 01:24:18.000
you might do this, or
cycling you might do this

01:24:18.000 --> 01:24:19.710
by focusing on a particular location

01:24:19.710 --> 01:24:22.543
and really homing in on
that location physically.

01:24:22.543 --> 01:24:25.380
And this is a practice that a
lot of athletes use in fact,

01:24:25.380 --> 01:24:28.500
and if you're say doing
musical practice or math,

01:24:28.500 --> 01:24:30.210
well then, you'd want
to focus on something

01:24:30.210 --> 01:24:32.520
other than the task that
you're trying to perform.

01:24:32.520 --> 01:24:34.500
But again, using visual focus

01:24:34.500 --> 01:24:38.160
as a way to ramp up and
increase your overall ability

01:24:38.160 --> 01:24:39.540
to focus and concentrate,

01:24:39.540 --> 01:24:41.460
and then applying that to whatever it is

01:24:41.460 --> 01:24:43.500
that you're trying to learn or perform.

01:24:43.500 --> 01:24:45.090
Next, I'd like to talk about compounds

01:24:45.090 --> 01:24:47.192
that can improve concentration and focus,

01:24:47.192 --> 01:24:50.130
and these are most often
consumed as supplements,

01:24:50.130 --> 01:24:51.720
although some of them, I should mention,

01:24:51.720 --> 01:24:54.030
can also be derived from food.

01:24:54.030 --> 01:24:55.363
Again, I just want to remind you

01:24:55.363 --> 01:24:57.990
that there are things,
in this case compounds,

01:24:57.990 --> 01:25:00.660
that can modulate a biological mechanism,

01:25:00.660 --> 01:25:03.420
that is can modulate
focus and concentration,

01:25:03.420 --> 01:25:06.750
and there are compounds that can mediate,

01:25:06.750 --> 01:25:10.723
can directly contribute to
concentration and focus.

01:25:10.723 --> 01:25:12.600
One of the key compounds

01:25:12.600 --> 01:25:14.880
that supports concentration and focus

01:25:14.880 --> 01:25:18.390
because it generally supports
mood, concentration and focus

01:25:18.390 --> 01:25:20.820
and brain function in general

01:25:20.820 --> 01:25:23.340
are the omega-3 essential fatty acids.

01:25:23.340 --> 01:25:25.920
I've talked about the
omega-3 essential fatty acids

01:25:25.920 --> 01:25:27.840
in a variety of contexts,
in particular depression,

01:25:27.840 --> 01:25:31.287
but also ADHD, there are
interesting data on that,

01:25:31.287 --> 01:25:32.441
and it's really clear

01:25:32.441 --> 01:25:36.564
that getting somewhere between
one and three grams of EPA,

01:25:36.564 --> 01:25:37.824
that is one to three grams

01:25:37.824 --> 01:25:42.824
of EPA essential fatty acid
per day can improve outcomes,

01:25:43.020 --> 01:25:46.740
that is can improve mood and
can improve cognitive function.

01:25:46.740 --> 01:25:47.579
And while there's some debate

01:25:47.579 --> 01:25:50.670
about whether or not it can
improve cardiac function,

01:25:50.670 --> 01:25:52.740
it's very clear, at least to me,

01:25:52.740 --> 01:25:54.870
that ingesting one to three grams

01:25:54.870 --> 01:25:57.990
of EPA essential fatty
acid per day is beneficial.

01:25:57.990 --> 01:26:00.930
But again, in the context
of focus and concentration,

01:26:00.930 --> 01:26:04.830
it's in modulating the neural
circuits and brain function

01:26:04.830 --> 01:26:07.140
that are going to support
focus and concentration.

01:26:07.140 --> 01:26:09.750
It's not as if taking one to three grams

01:26:09.750 --> 01:26:12.090
of EPA essential fatty acid per day

01:26:12.090 --> 01:26:14.580
is going to tap directly into

01:26:14.580 --> 01:26:17.013
only the circuits for
focus and concentration.

01:26:17.940 --> 01:26:20.730
That said, and as discussed on the episode

01:26:20.730 --> 01:26:23.580
of the Huberman Lab Podcast
with Dr. Rhonda Patrick,

01:26:23.580 --> 01:26:26.824
and on the episode on ADHD that I did,

01:26:26.824 --> 01:26:28.590
and on the episode on
depression that I did,

01:26:28.590 --> 01:26:31.350
I make it a point to
ingest one to three grams

01:26:31.350 --> 01:26:33.570
of EPAs per day.

01:26:33.570 --> 01:26:35.760
You can get those EPAs

01:26:35.760 --> 01:26:39.000
from other sources besides
supplements, of course,

01:26:39.000 --> 01:26:41.220
but supplements are going to
be the easiest way to do that.

01:26:41.220 --> 01:26:42.630
You could do that through liquid form,

01:26:42.630 --> 01:26:44.220
fish oil, cod liver oil,

01:26:44.220 --> 01:26:47.220
some people who are vegan opt
for other sources of EPAs,

01:26:47.220 --> 01:26:48.810
you can find those out there certainly.

01:26:48.810 --> 01:26:50.670
Some people even use prescription EPAs

01:26:50.670 --> 01:26:53.100
to get the dosage really high.

01:26:53.100 --> 01:26:55.710
Dr. Ronda Patrick talked about
this in the episode with me,

01:26:55.710 --> 01:26:57.150
that's actually something that she does.

01:26:57.150 --> 01:26:58.440
I don't take the prescription form,

01:26:58.440 --> 01:27:00.390
I get them through pill form

01:27:00.390 --> 01:27:03.600
through our supplement
affiliate, which is Momentous,

01:27:03.600 --> 01:27:05.940
but there are a number of
different quality sources

01:27:05.940 --> 01:27:07.424
of EPAs out there.

01:27:07.424 --> 01:27:09.900
And some of those quality
sources also include things

01:27:09.900 --> 01:27:12.630
like fatty fish, algae,
and things of that sort.

01:27:12.630 --> 01:27:14.880
So I'll leave it to you as to
whether or not you supplement

01:27:14.880 --> 01:27:16.290
with omega-3 fatty acids

01:27:16.290 --> 01:27:17.940
in order to get that one
to three grams per day

01:27:17.940 --> 01:27:19.440
or whether or not you do it through food,

01:27:19.440 --> 01:27:22.172
but I would encourage you to
try and reach that threshold

01:27:22.172 --> 01:27:25.410
because there are a number
of known positive effects

01:27:25.410 --> 01:27:27.660
for mood and brain function generally.

01:27:27.660 --> 01:27:31.020
The other thing that can
positively modulate brain function

01:27:31.020 --> 01:27:35.100
and that actually works as a
fuel for neurons to function

01:27:35.100 --> 01:27:36.720
and can improve cognitive performance,

01:27:36.720 --> 01:27:38.880
and particularly within
the brain circuits,

01:27:38.880 --> 01:27:40.170
such as the prefrontal cortex,

01:27:40.170 --> 01:27:43.290
that are involved in concentration
and focus is creatine.

01:27:43.290 --> 01:27:45.870
I know many people are familiar
with creatine monohydrate

01:27:45.870 --> 01:27:48.420
for its effects on muscle growth

01:27:48.420 --> 01:27:50.100
and strength and performance,

01:27:50.100 --> 01:27:52.590
but it's quite clear that the
bulk of scientific studies

01:27:52.590 --> 01:27:55.680
have examined the role of
creatine in the clinical context

01:27:55.680 --> 01:27:58.500
and as its role in improving
cognitive performance.

01:27:58.500 --> 01:28:00.660
So my read of the literature
has led to a practice

01:28:00.660 --> 01:28:03.030
in which I ingest five grams per day

01:28:03.030 --> 01:28:04.650
of creatine monohydrate,

01:28:04.650 --> 01:28:07.470
the sort of standard
form that's available in,

01:28:07.470 --> 01:28:09.390
this is generally available as a powder,

01:28:09.390 --> 01:28:10.223
that's certainly how I take it.

01:28:10.223 --> 01:28:11.940
I'll take the creatine powder,

01:28:11.940 --> 01:28:14.310
I'll mix it with water or
with my Athletic Greens

01:28:14.310 --> 01:28:16.080
or some sort of electrolyte drink,

01:28:16.080 --> 01:28:18.930
whatever liquid happens to be
convenient to ingest that in,

01:28:18.930 --> 01:28:20.820
the time of day doesn't
really seem to be important.

01:28:20.820 --> 01:28:21.990
Some people are strong believers

01:28:21.990 --> 01:28:25.140
in consuming creatine post-workout.

01:28:25.140 --> 01:28:26.460
While that might be beneficial,

01:28:26.460 --> 01:28:29.730
I simply take it in the
morning or post-workout,

01:28:29.730 --> 01:28:32.310
it sort of depends on when
I remember to take it,

01:28:32.310 --> 01:28:34.740
but that five grams of creatine per day,

01:28:34.740 --> 01:28:37.260
in my case, really isn't
geared towards muscle growth

01:28:37.260 --> 01:28:38.760
or strength or performance

01:28:38.760 --> 01:28:40.350
as much as it's geared toward

01:28:40.350 --> 01:28:43.620
tapping into the creatine
phosphate system within the brain

01:28:43.620 --> 01:28:46.920
and specifically the benefits of creatine

01:28:46.920 --> 01:28:49.740
for prefrontal cortical
networks, again, modulating,

01:28:49.740 --> 01:28:50.760
not directly mediating,

01:28:50.760 --> 01:28:53.280
but modulating and generally supporting

01:28:53.280 --> 01:28:55.830
the brain networks that
are going to allow me

01:28:55.830 --> 01:28:58.230
to generate focus and concentration.

01:28:58.230 --> 01:29:01.950
So much like sleep, much
like omega-3 fatty acids,

01:29:01.950 --> 01:29:04.020
creatine monohydrate five grams a day

01:29:04.020 --> 01:29:06.180
seems to generally support brain function,

01:29:06.180 --> 01:29:09.750
which will generally support
concentration and focus.

01:29:09.750 --> 01:29:11.310
Now in terms of compounds

01:29:11.310 --> 01:29:14.640
that more specifically mediate
concentration and focus,

01:29:14.640 --> 01:29:16.740
we have to go back to
that arrow metaphor model

01:29:16.740 --> 01:29:18.750
that we talked about at the
beginning of the episode,

01:29:18.750 --> 01:29:22.950
that included epinephrine,
adrenaline, acetylcholine,

01:29:22.950 --> 01:29:24.990
which acts as this attentional spotlight,

01:29:24.990 --> 01:29:28.290
in fact, acetylcholine and
elevated levels of acetylcholine

01:29:28.290 --> 01:29:30.030
have been shown over and over again

01:29:30.030 --> 01:29:33.120
through beautiful work from
Mike Merzenich's lab at UCSF

01:29:33.120 --> 01:29:36.090
and the Kilgard Lab down in Houston,

01:29:36.090 --> 01:29:37.320
and a number of other labs,

01:29:37.320 --> 01:29:40.050
including Norm Weinberger's
lab at UC Irvine,

01:29:40.050 --> 01:29:41.310
again and again,

01:29:41.310 --> 01:29:46.255
to improve or even directly
gate neuroplasticity

01:29:46.255 --> 01:29:49.410
by increasing focus directly.

01:29:49.410 --> 01:29:50.430
That's a lot of word soup,

01:29:50.430 --> 01:29:51.750
but basically what happens is

01:29:51.750 --> 01:29:53.430
if acetylcholine transmission

01:29:53.430 --> 01:29:56.070
is increased even
transiently within the brain,

01:29:56.070 --> 01:29:57.630
there's a greater opportunity

01:29:57.630 --> 01:29:59.550
for neuroplasticity to take place.

01:29:59.550 --> 01:30:01.890
And the reason there's
a greater opportunity

01:30:01.890 --> 01:30:05.160
for neuroplasticity, AKA
learning to take place,

01:30:05.160 --> 01:30:08.070
is by way of the increased focus

01:30:08.070 --> 01:30:11.010
that spiking acetylcholine can provide.

01:30:11.010 --> 01:30:11.843
As I mentioned earlier,

01:30:11.843 --> 01:30:14.040
there are a number of different
foods which contain choline,

01:30:14.040 --> 01:30:15.310
you can look those up online,

01:30:15.310 --> 01:30:18.840
choline acting as an amino acid
precursor to acetylcholine,

01:30:18.840 --> 01:30:20.610
but of course, there are
compounds, there are supplements

01:30:20.610 --> 01:30:23.400
that can further and more
acutely increase acetylcholine,

01:30:23.400 --> 01:30:25.080
and indeed, I use these myself.

01:30:25.080 --> 01:30:28.650
The most effective one
I've found is Alpha-GPC.

01:30:28.650 --> 01:30:31.710
Alpha-GPC consumed at dosages

01:30:31.710 --> 01:30:34.590
of 300 milligrams to 600 milligrams

01:30:34.590 --> 01:30:37.410
prior to a work about
or prior to a workout

01:30:37.410 --> 01:30:40.620
greatly increase one's ability
to focus and concentrate,

01:30:40.620 --> 01:30:42.000
at least that's been my experience,

01:30:42.000 --> 01:30:44.280
and there are some good data in humans.

01:30:44.280 --> 01:30:46.230
So how would I use Alpha-GPC?

01:30:46.230 --> 01:30:49.980
I would use Alpha-GPC by taking
it about 10 to 20 minutes

01:30:49.980 --> 01:30:52.590
prior to any time I want to focus

01:30:52.590 --> 01:30:54.720
or concentrate very deeply.

01:30:54.720 --> 01:30:57.870
I've taken as much as 600
milligrams at one time,

01:30:57.870 --> 01:31:00.090
although I find that 300
milligrams is enough for me,

01:31:00.090 --> 01:31:02.700
and I tend to be quite
sensitive to supplements

01:31:02.700 --> 01:31:04.050
and caffeine in general,

01:31:04.050 --> 01:31:07.470
so I'll sometimes take
it alongside yerba mate

01:31:07.470 --> 01:31:10.800
or with yerba mate or with
coffee prior to a workout

01:31:10.800 --> 01:31:12.810
or prior to a about of work

01:31:12.810 --> 01:31:15.270
in which I'm focusing on mental work.

01:31:15.270 --> 01:31:16.620
So it could be reading, writing,

01:31:16.620 --> 01:31:18.330
could be math, could be data analysis,

01:31:18.330 --> 01:31:19.163
could be anything

01:31:19.163 --> 01:31:21.000
where I need a lot of
focus and concentration.

01:31:21.000 --> 01:31:22.620
Now a number of people have contacted me

01:31:22.620 --> 01:31:26.880
about a recent study
suggesting that Alpha-GPC

01:31:26.880 --> 01:31:28.680
when taken chronically over many years

01:31:28.680 --> 01:31:32.160
could increase one's
vulnerability to stroke.

01:31:32.160 --> 01:31:35.408
I've looked at those data
and my read of the data

01:31:35.408 --> 01:31:37.620
is that they're not very conclusive,

01:31:37.620 --> 01:31:40.693
although anytime you
see something like that,

01:31:40.693 --> 01:31:42.393
a study that's pointing to the fact

01:31:42.393 --> 01:31:43.590
that a given compound

01:31:43.590 --> 01:31:45.780
might increase the propensity for stroke,

01:31:45.780 --> 01:31:47.370
you obviously want to be concerned.

01:31:47.370 --> 01:31:50.670
So we have to ask ourselves
how, by what mechanism that is,

01:31:50.670 --> 01:31:54.750
could Alpha-GPC be increasing
the susceptibility to stroke?

01:31:54.750 --> 01:31:57.960
And it seems to be related
to increases in TMAO,

01:31:57.960 --> 01:32:01.209
which is a marker related to
the cardiovascular system.

01:32:01.209 --> 01:32:06.180
And one known way to
offset increases in TMAO

01:32:06.180 --> 01:32:08.490
that are associated either with Alpha-GPC

01:32:08.490 --> 01:32:11.550
or increases due to other things,

01:32:11.550 --> 01:32:13.890
so ingestion of particular food compounds

01:32:13.890 --> 01:32:15.690
actually can increase TMAO,

01:32:15.690 --> 01:32:20.160
is to offset that by taking
600 milligrams of garlic.

01:32:20.160 --> 01:32:21.894
Now I've been taking Alpha-GPC

01:32:21.894 --> 01:32:23.940
pretty consistently for a number of years.

01:32:23.940 --> 01:32:25.260
I do not take it every day.

01:32:25.260 --> 01:32:27.360
I would say I take it
about four days per week,

01:32:27.360 --> 01:32:30.420
again, prior to workouts
or bouts of cognitive work.

01:32:30.420 --> 01:32:33.030
I have not seen my TMAO spike

01:32:33.030 --> 01:32:36.420
and I've evaluated that
by way of blood tests,

01:32:36.420 --> 01:32:39.390
but nonetheless, I take
600 milligrams of garlic

01:32:39.390 --> 01:32:42.480
in capsule form anytime I eat anyway

01:32:42.480 --> 01:32:44.640
and I do that for general
cardiovascular function

01:32:44.640 --> 01:32:45.660
and there's some interesting data

01:32:45.660 --> 01:32:48.660
on immune system function,
et cetera for garlic.

01:32:48.660 --> 01:32:52.620
So I've been consuming 600
milligram capsules of garlic

01:32:52.620 --> 01:32:53.730
for some period of time.

01:32:53.730 --> 01:32:56.580
Some days, I'll ingest just
one 600 milligram capsule,

01:32:56.580 --> 01:32:57.900
other times, I'll take two,

01:32:57.900 --> 01:33:01.943
but based on this recent study
and the concerns about TMAO,

01:33:01.943 --> 01:33:03.570
I make it a point

01:33:03.570 --> 01:33:07.500
to always ingest a 600
milligram capsule of garlic

01:33:07.500 --> 01:33:09.090
anytime I take Alpha-GPC,

01:33:09.090 --> 01:33:11.670
which again for me is
about four days per week.

01:33:11.670 --> 01:33:14.051
So in our model of attention and focus,

01:33:14.051 --> 01:33:16.950
you can now clearly see
why taking Alpha-GPC,

01:33:16.950 --> 01:33:19.170
which increases acetylcholine transmission

01:33:19.170 --> 01:33:21.570
would be beneficial for
concentration and focus

01:33:21.570 --> 01:33:24.360
and why taking it with a double espresso

01:33:24.360 --> 01:33:26.700
or why taking it with yerba mate

01:33:26.700 --> 01:33:29.790
would further increase
concentration and focus

01:33:29.790 --> 01:33:31.156
because as I mentioned earlier,

01:33:31.156 --> 01:33:33.918
caffeine is going to increase epinephrine.

01:33:33.918 --> 01:33:36.330
It's also going to increase the density

01:33:36.330 --> 01:33:37.980
of dopamine receptors

01:33:37.980 --> 01:33:40.140
and the Alpha-GPC is going
to increase acetylcholine,

01:33:40.140 --> 01:33:41.640
this spotlighting for cognition,

01:33:41.640 --> 01:33:44.550
this ability to really
amplify the activity

01:33:44.550 --> 01:33:45.930
of specific neural networks,

01:33:45.930 --> 01:33:47.610
which is largely what's happening

01:33:47.610 --> 01:33:49.260
when you're trying to focus

01:33:49.260 --> 01:33:51.660
and pay attention to
something specifically.

01:33:51.660 --> 01:33:53.160
So if one wants to increase

01:33:53.160 --> 01:33:56.460
the amount of dopamine
transmission in the brain and body

01:33:56.460 --> 01:33:59.400
for sake of increasing
concentration and focus,

01:33:59.400 --> 01:34:01.410
one of the most efficient ways to do that

01:34:01.410 --> 01:34:03.720
is by ingestion of the
amino acid L-tyrosine.

01:34:03.720 --> 01:34:06.810
Again, L-tyrosine can be
derived from food sources,

01:34:06.810 --> 01:34:07.710
I invite you to look up

01:34:07.710 --> 01:34:09.090
those various food sources on the web,

01:34:09.090 --> 01:34:10.770
simply go to a web browser and put in

01:34:10.770 --> 01:34:12.420
foods that contain a lot of L-tyrosine

01:34:12.420 --> 01:34:14.996
and you'll get a rich array
of choices to select from.

01:34:14.996 --> 01:34:19.470
But in my case, I use
L-tyrosine in capsule form.

01:34:19.470 --> 01:34:21.734
I will take 500 milligrams of L-tyrosine,

01:34:21.734 --> 01:34:25.170
300 milligrams of Alpha-GPC
and a cup of coffee.

01:34:25.170 --> 01:34:27.210
I'm careful to do this early in the day,

01:34:27.210 --> 01:34:28.920
certainly not after two or 3:00 PM

01:34:28.920 --> 01:34:31.020
because I don't want
to diminish my ability

01:34:31.020 --> 01:34:32.626
to fall and stay asleep that night.

01:34:32.626 --> 01:34:34.740
I'll do this early in
the day before a workout

01:34:34.740 --> 01:34:38.444
or before a about of
concentrated mental work.

01:34:38.444 --> 01:34:41.730
Again, I tend to do this
about four days per week,

01:34:41.730 --> 01:34:45.311
so certainly not every
time I sit down to do work.

01:34:45.311 --> 01:34:47.730
And I should also mention

01:34:47.730 --> 01:34:49.650
that I still tend to do
the behavioral tools.

01:34:49.650 --> 01:34:51.780
I'll tend to use five
minutes of binaural beats

01:34:51.780 --> 01:34:53.682
or binaural beats
throughout the work session,

01:34:53.682 --> 01:34:56.498
sometimes do a ice bath
or a cold shower before.

01:34:56.498 --> 01:34:58.320
I don't want to give the impression

01:34:58.320 --> 01:35:01.740
that I combine every tool
that I've talked about today

01:35:01.740 --> 01:35:02.940
for a given workout.

01:35:02.940 --> 01:35:05.850
I mean, that would be pretty
wild to take a cold shower,

01:35:05.850 --> 01:35:08.190
pop an L-tyrosine, take an Alpha-GPC,

01:35:08.190 --> 01:35:10.350
drink two espresso,
listen to binaural beats.

01:35:10.350 --> 01:35:13.440
That to me seems like
a very inefficient way

01:35:13.440 --> 01:35:14.334
to go about life.

01:35:14.334 --> 01:35:18.930
In fact, I make it a
point to try and use tools

01:35:18.930 --> 01:35:21.420
to increase my ability
to concentrate and focus,

01:35:21.420 --> 01:35:24.060
but not to combine more
than two or three of them

01:35:24.060 --> 01:35:24.893
at any one time.

01:35:24.893 --> 01:35:25.800
And when I say two or three,

01:35:25.800 --> 01:35:28.667
what I mean is I will use
supplements like Alpha-GPC,

01:35:28.667 --> 01:35:31.800
L-Tyrosine and caffeine together
before certain work bouts,

01:35:31.800 --> 01:35:33.720
I might use the visual practice

01:35:33.720 --> 01:35:35.490
of focusing on a given
location for a minute

01:35:35.490 --> 01:35:37.002
before I begin that work about,

01:35:37.002 --> 01:35:38.940
I might combine those.

01:35:38.940 --> 01:35:41.730
Then another time I
might take a cold shower

01:35:41.730 --> 01:35:43.110
prior to doing some work.

01:35:43.110 --> 01:35:45.810
Other days, I confess,
I've slept very well

01:35:45.810 --> 01:35:49.530
or my enthusiasm about what
I'm about to work on is such

01:35:49.530 --> 01:35:51.480
that I don't require any of these tools.

01:35:51.480 --> 01:35:53.820
Again, there's no requirement,

01:35:53.820 --> 01:35:55.800
there's no pressure to
use any of these tools,

01:35:55.800 --> 01:35:58.375
behavioral, supplement based or otherwise.

01:35:58.375 --> 01:36:02.010
It's simply a matter of using the tools

01:36:02.010 --> 01:36:02.970
that are going to allow you

01:36:02.970 --> 01:36:04.470
to achieve the states you want to achieve

01:36:04.470 --> 01:36:07.860
and to improve your ability
to go into those states

01:36:07.860 --> 01:36:09.210
without any help at all.

01:36:09.210 --> 01:36:10.620
And this is what I find

01:36:10.620 --> 01:36:12.540
particularly attractive about supplements.

01:36:12.540 --> 01:36:14.070
It's not so much that they put you

01:36:14.070 --> 01:36:16.290
into the ideal state for that work

01:36:16.290 --> 01:36:18.150
and then you accomplish that work

01:36:18.150 --> 01:36:20.044
and then you always rely
on those supplements.

01:36:20.044 --> 01:36:22.260
I prefer to look at supplements

01:36:22.260 --> 01:36:23.910
of the sort that I just described

01:36:23.910 --> 01:36:27.561
as a route into a deeper trench
of focus and concentration

01:36:27.561 --> 01:36:30.780
that I use as a tool to teach myself

01:36:30.780 --> 01:36:32.400
to focus and concentrate more deeply,

01:36:32.400 --> 01:36:34.470
such that I don't need those tools

01:36:34.470 --> 01:36:37.403
every single time I try
and focus and concentrate.

01:36:37.403 --> 01:36:39.390
I think this is an important point

01:36:39.390 --> 01:36:41.850
because I think that many
people think of supplements

01:36:41.850 --> 01:36:46.850
as a crutch or a way of
simply getting into a state

01:36:46.877 --> 01:36:50.850
for which no other tool
will suffice or replace.

01:36:50.850 --> 01:36:52.560
But in that context,

01:36:52.560 --> 01:36:55.200
I want to remind you of the
larger context of pharmacology,

01:36:55.200 --> 01:36:57.000
which is the vast landscape

01:36:57.000 --> 01:36:59.580
of prescription pharmacology for ADHD,

01:36:59.580 --> 01:37:01.860
for attention deficit
hyperactivity disorder.

01:37:01.860 --> 01:37:05.370
Now I covered that
landscape in intense detail

01:37:05.370 --> 01:37:07.754
on the episode on ADHD and focus.

01:37:07.754 --> 01:37:11.450
And just to summarize, there is of course,

01:37:11.450 --> 01:37:16.050
Adderall, Ritalin, Vyvanse,
Modafinil, Armodafinil,

01:37:16.050 --> 01:37:17.031
a number of different compounds,

01:37:17.031 --> 01:37:21.120
all of which generally
increase dopamine transmission

01:37:21.120 --> 01:37:22.710
in the brain, so increasing dopamine,

01:37:22.710 --> 01:37:25.260
and all of which generally
increase epinephrine,

01:37:25.260 --> 01:37:27.544
adrenaline transmission
in the brain and body.

01:37:27.544 --> 01:37:30.360
And many of those compounds

01:37:30.360 --> 01:37:32.520
have been of tremendous benefit

01:37:32.520 --> 01:37:35.490
to children and even some
adults who suffer from ADHD.

01:37:35.490 --> 01:37:38.910
So properly prescribed at
the appropriate dosage,

01:37:38.910 --> 01:37:41.910
those compounds can really help people

01:37:41.910 --> 01:37:44.309
with clinically diagnosed ADHD.

01:37:44.309 --> 01:37:48.270
The way they help those people
is a bit surprising, however.

01:37:48.270 --> 01:37:50.820
You might think, well they
turn on the brain chemicals

01:37:50.820 --> 01:37:53.080
that allow those people
to concentrate and focus.

01:37:53.080 --> 01:37:54.245
That's true,

01:37:54.245 --> 01:37:56.610
but they also have the benefit

01:37:56.610 --> 01:38:00.510
of teaching those brain
circuits how to engage.

01:38:00.510 --> 01:38:04.170
And that's one of the reasons
why somewhat paradoxically

01:38:04.170 --> 01:38:07.470
giving a stimulant like
Ritalin or Adderall

01:38:07.470 --> 01:38:09.960
to a kid that legitimately needs it,

01:38:09.960 --> 01:38:12.690
obviously, you don't want to
do this without the oversight

01:38:12.690 --> 01:38:15.810
and careful evaluation of a psychiatrist,

01:38:15.810 --> 01:38:19.094
but giving that to a
kid who has severe ADHD,

01:38:19.094 --> 01:38:22.230
you would think would make
them more rambunctious,

01:38:22.230 --> 01:38:24.810
less able to focus and
more distractible overall.

01:38:24.810 --> 01:38:28.137
After all, Ritalin,
Modafinil, Armodafinil,

01:38:28.137 --> 01:38:29.490
all these things are stimulants.

01:38:29.490 --> 01:38:30.420
So you take a kid

01:38:30.420 --> 01:38:33.300
who has attention deficit
hyperactivity disorder

01:38:33.300 --> 01:38:35.190
and give them these drugs

01:38:35.190 --> 01:38:37.470
that increase transmission
of dopamine and epinephrine

01:38:37.470 --> 01:38:38.760
and you think, wow,
it's going to make them

01:38:38.760 --> 01:38:40.560
even more distractible and hyperactive

01:38:40.560 --> 01:38:42.210
and indeed, it has the opposite effect.

01:38:42.210 --> 01:38:44.055
It doesn't necessarily
make them feel calm,

01:38:44.055 --> 01:38:47.460
but it makes them feel
that they can focus,

01:38:47.460 --> 01:38:49.470
they really can anchor their attention.

01:38:49.470 --> 01:38:52.231
And the idea is that it's
teaching those neural circuits

01:38:52.231 --> 01:38:55.320
or those neural circuits
rather are teaching themselves

01:38:55.320 --> 01:38:57.720
to engage and to focus and concentrate.

01:38:57.720 --> 01:38:59.580
And the ideal situation is one in which

01:38:59.580 --> 01:39:02.460
the total dosage of those
compounds, those drugs,

01:39:02.460 --> 01:39:03.720
can be reduced over time

01:39:03.720 --> 01:39:05.880
as those circuits learn to come online

01:39:05.880 --> 01:39:07.230
through purely behavioral tools.

01:39:07.230 --> 01:39:09.690
Now oftentimes, there's a
maintenance of those drugs

01:39:09.690 --> 01:39:10.890
over long periods of time,

01:39:10.890 --> 01:39:12.720
although there is a
common practice nowadays

01:39:12.720 --> 01:39:15.029
of trying to diminish the dosage overall.

01:39:15.029 --> 01:39:18.030
That's in the context of ADHD
and prescription medication

01:39:18.030 --> 01:39:19.706
and I acknowledge that a lot,

01:39:19.706 --> 01:39:24.706
indeed 80% or more of college
students say the statistics

01:39:25.643 --> 01:39:28.440
are using prescription drugs

01:39:28.440 --> 01:39:30.270
when they are not in fact

01:39:30.270 --> 01:39:32.040
prescribed those prescription drugs.

01:39:32.040 --> 01:39:33.570
So basically what I'm saying

01:39:33.570 --> 01:39:35.100
is there are a lot of people using drugs

01:39:35.100 --> 01:39:37.290
designed for ADHD and narcolepsy

01:39:37.290 --> 01:39:38.763
because those drugs

01:39:38.763 --> 01:39:41.940
will effectively increase
focus and concentration,

01:39:41.940 --> 01:39:44.490
but I strongly discourage

01:39:44.490 --> 01:39:46.980
the use of powerful prescription drugs

01:39:46.980 --> 01:39:48.210
that have not been prescribed to you.

01:39:48.210 --> 01:39:49.080
First of all, it's illegal.

01:39:49.080 --> 01:39:50.820
Second of all, it's quite dangerous

01:39:50.820 --> 01:39:53.790
to hit the accelerator
of those neural circuits

01:39:53.790 --> 01:39:55.230
with such vigor

01:39:55.230 --> 01:39:57.240
because it can increase dependency

01:39:57.240 --> 01:39:58.860
and they can have a number
of other side effects

01:39:58.860 --> 01:40:01.050
outside the context of
clinically diagnosed

01:40:01.050 --> 01:40:04.385
and prescribed ADHD medication.

01:40:04.385 --> 01:40:07.350
But in the context of supplementation,

01:40:07.350 --> 01:40:12.350
the increase in dopamine,
acetylcholine and epinephrine

01:40:12.450 --> 01:40:15.120
that one can achieve from say
500 milligrams of L-tyrosine,

01:40:15.120 --> 01:40:20.120
300 milligrams of Alpha-GPC
and a cup of coffee

01:40:20.190 --> 01:40:22.350
is going to be substantially less

01:40:22.350 --> 01:40:24.060
than one would see for prescription drugs.

01:40:24.060 --> 01:40:26.043
So you're getting a modest effect

01:40:26.043 --> 01:40:29.880
that can similarly teach
those brain circuits

01:40:29.880 --> 01:40:33.030
for focus and concentration
how to engage better.

01:40:33.030 --> 01:40:35.820
But as a general backdrop to all of this,

01:40:35.820 --> 01:40:38.400
I always say and I'll
say it again and again

01:40:38.400 --> 01:40:39.930
probably until the day I die,

01:40:39.930 --> 01:40:41.340
which hopefully is a long time from now,

01:40:41.340 --> 01:40:43.950
but regardless, it'll be the same message,

01:40:43.950 --> 01:40:46.923
I always believe that behavioral
tools should come first,

01:40:46.923 --> 01:40:48.660
behavioral tools should come first.

01:40:48.660 --> 01:40:50.250
Then focus on nutrition.

01:40:50.250 --> 01:40:52.500
In fact, I would say
behavioral and nutrition tools,

01:40:52.500 --> 01:40:54.635
and of course, get excellent sleep.

01:40:54.635 --> 01:40:58.866
Then focus on supplementation and then,

01:40:58.866 --> 01:41:01.830
and only if those are failing

01:41:01.830 --> 01:41:03.450
to bring your brain and body

01:41:03.450 --> 01:41:04.590
to the state you need to be in

01:41:04.590 --> 01:41:07.419
to perform well in school
and work and life, et cetera

01:41:07.419 --> 01:41:12.240
do I recommend that people
lean on prescription drugs.

01:41:12.240 --> 01:41:13.290
Now there's a caveat to that,

01:41:13.290 --> 01:41:16.521
which is in under conditions
like severe eating disorders,

01:41:16.521 --> 01:41:20.105
obsessive compulsive
disorder, bipolar disorder,

01:41:20.105 --> 01:41:25.105
depression where people are
truly at risk of suicide

01:41:25.530 --> 01:41:27.480
or severe mental health effects

01:41:27.480 --> 01:41:29.130
or behavioral health effects

01:41:29.130 --> 01:41:31.920
and they're really at,
their lives are at risk

01:41:31.920 --> 01:41:34.033
and their overall mood
and wellbeing is at risk,

01:41:34.033 --> 01:41:37.050
it's often the case that
people cannot access

01:41:37.050 --> 01:41:39.060
the brain states required

01:41:39.060 --> 01:41:41.250
to shift themselves purely
with behavioral tools,

01:41:41.250 --> 01:41:42.180
nutrition, et cetera.

01:41:42.180 --> 01:41:44.910
So again, for the typical person

01:41:44.910 --> 01:41:45.840
who's not suffering

01:41:45.840 --> 01:41:47.521
from one of the psychiatric disorders

01:41:47.521 --> 01:41:50.325
that I mentioned before or
other psychiatric disorders,

01:41:50.325 --> 01:41:52.550
schizophrenia, et cetera,

01:41:52.550 --> 01:41:56.760
I strongly encourage you to
look to behavioral tools first,

01:41:56.760 --> 01:41:59.610
nutrition, then supplementation,

01:41:59.610 --> 01:42:02.910
then and only if there's a remaining need,

01:42:02.910 --> 01:42:04.358
to prescription drugs.

01:42:04.358 --> 01:42:06.240
This contrasts very much

01:42:06.240 --> 01:42:08.460
with the typical scenario
I hear about these days

01:42:08.460 --> 01:42:10.890
where college students
or other people will say,

01:42:10.890 --> 01:42:12.641
oh yeah, I hear that there's this drug,

01:42:12.641 --> 01:42:15.797
Ritalin or Vyvanse that can
immediately put me into a state

01:42:15.797 --> 01:42:17.880
of heightened focus and concentration.

01:42:17.880 --> 01:42:19.680
Now listen, if you have ADHD,

01:42:19.680 --> 01:42:21.840
by all means, talk to a physician,

01:42:21.840 --> 01:42:23.430
talk to a great psychiatrist

01:42:23.430 --> 01:42:25.290
and figure out whether or
not that's right for you.

01:42:25.290 --> 01:42:26.520
But if you don't,

01:42:26.520 --> 01:42:29.167
again, behavioral tools,
nutrition, supplementation,

01:42:29.167 --> 01:42:32.910
and in particular, those behavioral tools

01:42:32.910 --> 01:42:35.130
are going to be the ones
that are going to allow you

01:42:35.130 --> 01:42:36.660
to teach your neural circuits

01:42:36.660 --> 01:42:38.130
how to focus and concentrate better,

01:42:38.130 --> 01:42:40.230
and I cannot overstate
the importance of that,

01:42:40.230 --> 01:42:41.400
that the behavioral tools,

01:42:41.400 --> 01:42:43.374
and to some extent the supplementation

01:42:43.374 --> 01:42:45.360
combined with behavioral tools

01:42:45.360 --> 01:42:48.630
really allow you to train
up your neural circuits

01:42:48.630 --> 01:42:50.687
so that you can focus and concentrate

01:42:50.687 --> 01:42:53.550
to the depth and the
degree and the duration

01:42:53.550 --> 01:42:56.430
that's going to best serve
your mental and physical goals.

01:42:56.430 --> 01:42:57.282
Now there's one other compound

01:42:57.282 --> 01:43:00.418
that I've used from time to
time and that I continue to use

01:43:00.418 --> 01:43:03.327
in order to increase
focus and concentration

01:43:03.327 --> 01:43:06.120
and I will use this in combination

01:43:06.120 --> 01:43:08.010
with the other supplements
I talked about before

01:43:08.010 --> 01:43:09.780
and that's phenylethylamine.

01:43:09.780 --> 01:43:12.780
Phenylethylamine is in the
dopamine synthesis pathway,

01:43:12.780 --> 01:43:14.610
so it increases dopamine transmission

01:43:14.610 --> 01:43:16.560
and tends to function a
little bit differently

01:43:16.560 --> 01:43:17.442
than L-tyrosine.

01:43:17.442 --> 01:43:20.850
So every once in a while,
I'll swap out L-tyrosine

01:43:20.850 --> 01:43:23.850
and put in 500 milligrams
of phenylethylamine

01:43:23.850 --> 01:43:27.210
or sometimes, if I really want
to push a little bit harder

01:43:27.210 --> 01:43:28.356
on the dopamine system,

01:43:28.356 --> 01:43:32.190
and I'm going to be doing a
long about of intense work,

01:43:32.190 --> 01:43:34.827
I will take the 300
milligrams of Alpha-GPC,

01:43:34.827 --> 01:43:37.350
the 500 milligrams of L-tyrosine,

01:43:37.350 --> 01:43:39.236
I'll generally take
that with some caffeine,

01:43:39.236 --> 01:43:40.770
and I should mention,

01:43:40.770 --> 01:43:43.560
I don't go past about 100 or
200 milligrams of caffeine

01:43:43.560 --> 01:43:45.360
'cause I don't really
like feeling too jittery,

01:43:45.360 --> 01:43:46.290
that's not really my goal,

01:43:46.290 --> 01:43:48.360
it's the goal to be alert,

01:43:48.360 --> 01:43:51.360
but not so alert that I really
can't focus on anything,

01:43:51.360 --> 01:43:54.480
I'm not interested in having
an anxiety attack after all,

01:43:54.480 --> 01:43:56.790
but I'll sometimes either swap in

01:43:56.790 --> 01:43:59.874
or I will add that 500
milligrams of phenylethylamine.

01:43:59.874 --> 01:44:02.963
Phenylethylamine is in the PEA pathway.

01:44:02.963 --> 01:44:04.110
I've talked about this

01:44:04.110 --> 01:44:07.140
in a previous podcast on
dopamine motivation and drive,

01:44:07.140 --> 01:44:10.530
and it's a very short-lived compound.

01:44:10.530 --> 01:44:12.066
So what I'll tend to do is take it

01:44:12.066 --> 01:44:13.950
once at the beginning of the workout

01:44:13.950 --> 01:44:15.930
and sometimes in the
middle of the workout,

01:44:15.930 --> 01:44:18.120
I'll take another 500 milligram capsule.

01:44:18.120 --> 01:44:19.170
But what I just described

01:44:19.170 --> 01:44:21.180
with combining all of those compounds,

01:44:21.180 --> 01:44:25.332
Alpha-GPC, L-tyrosine,
phenylethylamine and caffeine,

01:44:25.332 --> 01:44:28.290
that's a fairly rare occurrence
that I'll combine all four

01:44:28.290 --> 01:44:29.610
and really only under conditions

01:44:29.610 --> 01:44:32.940
in which I have to do an
intensely challenging about

01:44:32.940 --> 01:44:34.830
of mental or physical work.

01:44:34.830 --> 01:44:36.630
I would say the frequency
at which I combine

01:44:36.630 --> 01:44:38.190
all four of those things

01:44:38.190 --> 01:44:40.860
is probably about once every two weeks,

01:44:40.860 --> 01:44:43.140
and typically more like once a month,

01:44:43.140 --> 01:44:46.290
again being careful to do that
in the early part of the day,

01:44:46.290 --> 01:44:48.390
certainly before the noon hour,

01:44:48.390 --> 01:44:50.970
so that I am in no way
going to disrupt my sleep.

01:44:50.970 --> 01:44:53.130
I realize that many of you
are probably wondering about

01:44:53.130 --> 01:44:54.540
or hoping that I'll discuss things

01:44:54.540 --> 01:44:57.150
like lions mane or the racetams

01:44:57.150 --> 01:44:59.520
or some of the other compounds

01:44:59.520 --> 01:45:01.440
that are known to powerfully modulate

01:45:01.440 --> 01:45:04.353
the dopamine, epinephrine
and acetylcholine systems.

01:45:05.190 --> 01:45:06.120
To be quite direct,

01:45:06.120 --> 01:45:08.010
there are far too many of these compounds

01:45:08.010 --> 01:45:09.780
to review in a single episode,

01:45:09.780 --> 01:45:13.560
and they all generally tap
into the same set of processes.

01:45:13.560 --> 01:45:16.530
Again, epinephrine, that
shaft of the arrowhead

01:45:16.530 --> 01:45:17.766
that we're thinking of as focus,

01:45:17.766 --> 01:45:20.520
acetylcholine, which is
the arrowhead itself,

01:45:20.520 --> 01:45:21.990
and then dopamine,

01:45:21.990 --> 01:45:24.630
which is the sort of
propeller behind the arrow

01:45:24.630 --> 01:45:26.910
that allows it to
continually drive forward

01:45:26.910 --> 01:45:29.553
through a about of
mental or physical work.

01:45:30.450 --> 01:45:31.710
There is a wonderful site.

01:45:31.710 --> 01:45:34.260
I've mentioned it several
times before on this podcast.

01:45:34.260 --> 01:45:36.540
That is examine.com.

01:45:36.540 --> 01:45:39.120
That wonderful site that is examine.com

01:45:39.120 --> 01:45:42.450
has recently been updated,
they've changed their format.

01:45:42.450 --> 01:45:43.950
It was terrific before,

01:45:43.950 --> 01:45:46.710
it provided links to relevant studies,

01:45:46.710 --> 01:45:48.210
it talked about specific compounds,

01:45:48.210 --> 01:45:49.770
it talked about the magnitude of effect,

01:45:49.770 --> 01:45:51.300
it talked about the human effect matrix,

01:45:51.300 --> 01:45:52.680
it really focused on human studies

01:45:52.680 --> 01:45:55.380
with links to those studies and on and on.

01:45:55.380 --> 01:45:58.980
The new revamped version of
examine.com is even better,

01:45:58.980 --> 01:46:00.690
it's really next, next level.

01:46:00.690 --> 01:46:03.450
I really applaud them for
doing such a terrific job

01:46:03.450 --> 01:46:04.710
in organizing the information.

01:46:04.710 --> 01:46:07.650
There are a lot of interesting
pages that you can read there

01:46:07.650 --> 01:46:08.790
about different compounds.

01:46:08.790 --> 01:46:11.370
So you can put in any
compound, ginko biloba,

01:46:11.370 --> 01:46:14.130
phosphatidylserine, Alpha-GPC,

01:46:14.130 --> 01:46:16.170
and you're going to get a
rich array of information

01:46:16.170 --> 01:46:17.490
about those compounds.

01:46:17.490 --> 01:46:19.890
And if you were to put
in a specific goal state,

01:46:19.890 --> 01:46:24.890
that is focus or concentration
or sleep or hormones,

01:46:25.140 --> 01:46:26.190
like testosterone, et cetera,

01:46:26.190 --> 01:46:28.530
you're going to get a rich array

01:46:28.530 --> 01:46:30.900
of compounds and supplements

